{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06383","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06383","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06383","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Lison","name":"Lison, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Protecting De-identified Documents from Search-based Linkage Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:02:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-16T20:06:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:12:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T15:29:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-22T01:17:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While de-identification models can help conceal the identity of the individuals mentioned in a document, they fail to address linkage risks, defined as the potential to map the de-identified text back to its source. One straightforward way to perform such linkages is to extract phrases from the de-identified document and check their presence in the original dataset. This paper presents a method to counter search-based linkage attacks while preserving the semantic integrity of the text. The method proceeds in two steps. We first construct an inverted index of the N-grams occurring in the text collection, making it possible to efficiently determine which N-grams appear in fewer than $k$ documents, either alone or in combination with other N-grams. An LLM-based rewriter is then iteratively queried to reformulate those spans until linkage is no longer possible. Experimental results on two datasets (court cases and Wikipedia biographies) show that the rewriting method can effectively prevent search-based linkages while remaining faithful to the original content. However, we also highlight that linkages remain feasible with the help of more advanced, semantics-oriented approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06383","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:01Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:00:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Hottung","name":"Hottung, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Berto","name":"Berto, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanbo","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Chuanbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nayeli Gast","familyName":"Zepeda","name":"Zepeda, Nayeli Gast","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Wetzel","name":"Wetzel, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Römer","name":"Römer, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Zago","name":"Zago, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Poli","name":"Poli, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Massaroli","name":"Massaroli, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinkyoo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jinkyoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Tierney","name":"Tierney, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VRPAgent: LLM-Driven Discovery of Heuristic Operators for Vehicle Routing Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:35:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T11:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T01:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing high-performing heuristics for vehicle routing problems (VRPs) is a complex task that requires both intuition and deep domain knowledge. Large language model (LLM)-based code generation has recently shown promise across many domains, but it still falls short of producing heuristics that rival those crafted by human experts. In this paper, we propose VRPAgent, a framework for VRPs that integrates LLM-generated components into a metaheuristic and refines them through a novel genetic search. By using the LLM to generate problem-specific operators, embedded within a shared metaheuristic framework for VRPs, VRPAgent keeps tasks manageable, guarantees correctness, and still enables the discovery of novel and powerful strategies. Across multiple VRPs, including the capacitated VRP, the VRP with time windows, and the prize-collecting VRP, our method discovers heuristic operators that outperform handcrafted methods and recent learning-based approaches while requiring only a single CPU core. To our knowledge, VRPAgent is one of the first LLM-based methods to advance the state-of-the-art in VRPs, highlighting a promising future for automated heuristics discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in TMLR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lekang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lekang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Goetz","name":"Goetz, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning for Hierarchical Text Classification: The Case of Patents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-17T07:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical text classification (HTC) assigns documents to multiple levels of a pre-defined taxonomy. Automated patent subject classification represents one of the hardest HTC scenarios because of professional difficulties and extensive labels. Prior approaches only output a flat label set, which offers little insight into the reason behind predictions. Therefore, we propose Reasoning for Hierarchical Classification (RHC), a novel framework that reformulates HTC as a step-by-step reasoning task to sequentially deduce hierarchical labels. RHC trains large language models (LLMs) in two stages: a cold-start stage that aligns outputs with chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning format and a reinforcement learning (RL) stage to enhance multi-step reasoning ability. RHC demonstrates four advantages in our experiments. (1) Effectiveness: RHC surpasses previous baselines and outperforms the supervised fine-tuning counterparts by approximately 3% in accuracy and macro F1. (2) Explainability: RHC produces natural-language justifications before prediction to facilitate human inspection. (3) Scalability: RHC scales favorably with model size with larger gains compared to standard fine-tuning. (4) Applicability: Beyond patents, we further demonstrate that RHC achieves state-of-the-art performance on other widely used HTC benchmarks, which highlights its broad applicability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings. 16 pages, 10 tables, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masih","familyName":"Aminbeidokhti","name":"Aminbeidokhti, Masih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heitor Rapela","familyName":"Medeiros","name":"Medeiros, Heitor Rapela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust and Efficient Finetuning of Vision Foundation Models via Implicit Ensembling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T15:33:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust finetuning of foundation models seeks to improve out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization during in-distribution (ID) adaptation. We revisit Mixout, a stochastic regularizer that intermittently replaces finetuned parameters with a reference anchor, and study its effectiveness for robust finetuning of vision foundation models. We reinterpret Mixout as a single-run, weight-sharing implicit ensemble and analyze its expected OOD error through a bias-variance-covariance-locality (BVCL) lens. This perspective reveals three key factors that govern the ID-OOD trade-off: the choice of masking anchor, the mask resampling frequency, and mask sparsity. Guided by this analysis, we propose GMixout, which generalizes Mixout in three ways. First, it explicitly controls the masking period through a resampling-frequency hyperparameter. Second, it replaces the fixed pretrained anchor with an exponential moving average snapshot that adapts throughout training. Third, it uses sparse kernels to update only a small subset of parameters at each iteration, introducing no inference-time overhead and enabling large-model finetuning on consumer-grade GPUs. Experiments across five vision benchmarks show that GMixout consistently outperforms Mixout, Model Soups, and strong parameter-efficient finetuning baselines under both covariate shift and class imbalance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 9 figures, Neurips 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziteng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized Multi-agent Social Simulation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T09:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:39:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent social interaction has clearly benefited from Large Language Models. However, current simulation systems still face challenges such as difficulties in scaling to diverse scenarios and poor reusability due to a lack of modular design. To address these issues, we designed and developed a modular, object-oriented framework that organically integrates various base classes through a hierarchical structure, harvesting scalability and reusability. We inherited the framework to realize common derived classes. Additionally, a memory summarization mechanism is proposed to filter and distill relevant information from raw memory data, prioritizing contextually salient events and interactions. By selecting and combining some necessary derived classes, we customized a specific simulated environment. Utilizing this simulated environment, we successfully simulated human interactions on social media, replicating real-world online social behaviors. The source code for the project will be released and evolve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donggyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Donggyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeok","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Hyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meeyoung","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Meeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungwon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sungwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyoon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sangyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jihee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EconCausal: A Context-Aware Economic Reasoning Benchmark for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:00:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T05:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:10:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T12:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:40:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Socio-economic causal effects depend heavily on their institutional and environmental contexts. The same intervention can produce different, even opposite, effects across regulatory regimes, market conditions, time periods, or populations. This poses a challenge for large language models (LLMs) in decision-support roles: can they infer the direction of a causal effect under a specified context, and revise that judgment when the context changes?\n To address this, we introduce EconCausal, a large-scale benchmark of 10,490 context-annotated causal triplets extracted from 2,595 high-quality empirical studies in top-tier economics and finance journals, constructed through a rigorous four-stage pipeline with multi-run consensus, context refinement, and multi-critic filtering.\n Across 19 models, we observe source-sign over-transfer in cross-context prediction. While top models reach ~84% accuracy in single-context prediction, mean accuracy is 74.75% overall but drops to 44.27% when source and target signs disagree. On these cases, accuracy is 11.54 percentage points (pp) lower with the signed source example than with the target input alone. Mean accuracy is below 60% under corrupted source signs. Models also struggle to predict study-reported nonsignificant effects (19.16% accuracy).\n The dataset and benchmark are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/econcausal-benchmark-6F12."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages. Revised version with updated evaluation results, robustness analyses, expert validation, and references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrestha","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Shrestha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Giordano","name":"Giordano, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan-Phong","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tuan-Phong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Razniewski","name":"Razniewski, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auditing a KB Elicitation of Frontier LLM Knowledge: A Multi-dimensional Analysis of GPTKB v1.5"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:48:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T07:23:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-18T13:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:54:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-13T13:17:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:50:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs are remarkable artifacts that have revolutionized a range of knowledge-intensive tasks. A significant contributor is their factual knowledge, which, to date, remains poorly understood, and is usually analyzed from biased samples. In this paper, we provide a framework and the results of a multi-dimensional analysis of GPTKB v1.5 (Hu et al., 2025a), a recursively elicited Knowledge Base (KB) of 100 million facts (or beliefs) of a frontier LLM, namely, GPT-4.1. Given the scale of the elicited facts, we provide a multi-dimensional approach to qualitatively and quantitatively analyze these facts as opposed to the mainstream fact completion benchmarks, which are prone to availability bias. We find that the models' factual knowledge differs quite significantly from established knowledge bases, and that its accuracy is significantly lower than indicated by previous benchmarks. We also find that inconsistency, ambiguity and hallucinations are major issues, shedding light on future research opportunities in neuro-symbolic AI concerning extraction, consolidation and verification of factual LLM knowledge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AKBC@EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06640","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06640","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06640","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat M.","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Nhat M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Do Xuan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Do Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong-Duy","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Cong-Duy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyan","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Chunyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min-Yen","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Min-Yen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luu Anh","familyName":"Tuan","name":"Tuan, Luu Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contextual Representation Flow Across Depth in State-Space and Transformer Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:46:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:24:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T04:15:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:17:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-13T12:51:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State Space Models (SSMs) have emerged as efficient, linear-time alternatives to Transformer-Based Models (TBMs) for long-context processing. However, their different mechanisms raise an important question: how does contextual representation flow across depth differ between these model families and their hybrids? We examine this question across twenty-one pretrained models, combining token-wise spectral diversity, layer-wise variation on seven datasets, and answer-location probing on two retrieval tasks. This joint analysis reveals that TBMs retain most of their token diversity at the output, whereas SSMs reach richer intermediate diversity but retain less of it on average, with late-layer compression. Comparisons with reinitialized weights reveal that these profiles depend on pretrained weights, while hybrids combine strong answer-location accessibility with architecture-dependent geometry, showing that high accessibility need not require the steadiest representation flow. Finally, we conduct theoretical case studies connecting these contrasts to mechanism-level differences: query-time token mixing in TBMs, repeated recurrent updates in SSMs, and preserved attention updates in hybrids."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06640","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07314","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07314","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07314","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Paischer","name":"Paischer, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianluca","familyName":"Galletti","name":"Galletti, Gianluca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Hornsby","name":"Hornsby, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Setinek","name":"Setinek, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Zanisi","name":"Zanisi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naomi","familyName":"Carey","name":"Carey, Naomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislas","familyName":"Pamela","name":"Pamela, Stanislas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Brandstetter","name":"Brandstetter, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GyroSwin: 5D Surrogates for Gyrokinetic Plasma Turbulence Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T11:45:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-19T08:22:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:36:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-04T13:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:49:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nuclear fusion plays a pivotal role in the quest for reliable and sustainable energy production. A major roadblock to viable fusion power is understanding plasma turbulence, which significantly impairs plasma confinement, and is vital for next-generation reactor design. Plasma turbulence is governed by the nonlinear gyrokinetic equation, which evolves a 5D distribution function over time. Due to its high computational cost, reduced-order models are often employed in practice to approximate turbulent transport of energy. However, they omit nonlinear effects unique to the full 5D dynamics. To tackle this, we introduce GyroSwin, the first scalable 5D neural surrogate that can model 5D nonlinear gyrokinetic simulations, thereby capturing the physical phenomena neglected by reduced models, while providing accurate estimates of turbulent heat transport. GyroSwin (i) extends hierarchical Vision Transformers to 5D, (ii) introduces cross-attention and integration modules for latent 3D$\\leftrightarrow$5D interactions between electrostatic potential fields and the distribution function, and (iii) performs channelwise mode separation inspired by nonlinear physics. We demonstrate that GyroSwin outperforms widely used reduced numerics on heat flux prediction, captures the turbulent energy cascade, and reduces the cost of fully resolved nonlinear gyrokinetics by three orders of magnitude while remaining physically verifiable. GyroSwin shows promising scaling laws, tested up to one billion parameters, paving the way for scalable neural surrogates for gyrokinetic simulations of plasma turbulence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025, First authors contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07314","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:15:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07277","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07277","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07277","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franco Javier","familyName":"Arellano","name":"Arellano, Franco Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José Ignacio","familyName":"Orlando","name":"Orlando, José Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Fundus-Specific Foundation Models for Diabetic Macular Edema Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:41:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-01T19:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T00:08:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diabetic Macular Edema (DME) is a leading cause of vision loss among patients with Diabetic Retinopathy (DR). While deep learning has shown promising results for automatically detecting this condition from fundus images, its application remains challenging due the limited availability of annotated data. Foundation Models (FM) have emerged as an alternative solution. However, it is unclear if they can cope with DME detection in particular. In this paper, we systematically compare different FM and standard transfer learning approaches for this task. Specifically, we compare the two most popular FM for retinal images-RETFound and FLAIR-and an EfficientNetB0 backbone, across different training regimes and evaluation settings in IDRiD, MESSIDOR-2 and OCT-and-Eye-FundusImages (OEFI). Results show that despite their scale, FM do not consistently outperform fine-tuned CNNs in this task. In particular, EfficientNet-B0 consistently achieves competitive or superior performance across evaluation settings, with FLAIR being the most competitive foundation model, consistently outperforming RETFound. These findings suggest that FMs do not necessarily provide an advantage for fine-grained ophthalmic tasks such as DME detection, even after fine-tuning, highlighting lightweight CNNs as strong baselines in data-scarce environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at SIPAIM 2025. v2: Corrected a labeling bug regarding DME positive class criteria. Experimental results updated after retraining; main conclusions remain unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07277","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:35:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong-En","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yong-En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chien","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yu-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Zi","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, An-Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Chih","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Wen-Chih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CARPAS: Towards Content-Aware Refinement of Provided Aspects for Summarization in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:16:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T17:15:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-03T01:11:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aspect-based summarization has attracted significant attention for its ability to generate more fine-grained and user-aligned summaries. While most existing approaches assume a set of predefined aspects as input, real-world scenarios often present challenges where these given aspects may be incomplete, irrelevant, or entirely missing from the document. Users frequently expect systems to adaptively refine or filter the provided aspects based on the actual content. In this paper, we initiate this novel task setting, termed Content-Aware Refinement of Provided Aspects for Summarization (CARPAS), with the aim of dynamically adjusting the provided aspects based on the document context before summarizing. We construct three new datasets and conduct pilot experiments with four representative prompting strategies. Our analysis reveals that LLMs often over-generate aspects, resulting in excessively long and misaligned summaries. Building on this observation, we introduce a two-stage framework that derives lightweight scope guidance before aspect refinement and summarization, improving focus and reducing over-generation. Our extensive experiments show that the proposed approach significantly improves performance across all datasets. Moreover, our deeper analyses uncover LLMs' compliance when the requested number of aspects differs from their own estimations, establishing a crucial insight for the deployment of LLMs in similar real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2026 FinNLP workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-03T02:35:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nian","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongzheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhongzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikun","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Allmendinger","name":"Allmendinger, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCCE: A Framework for Multi-LLM Collaborative Search in Discrete Spaces with Similarity-Filtered Preference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T07:42:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-objective discrete optimization problems, such as molecular design, pose significant challenges due to their vast and unstructured combinatorial spaces. Traditional evolutionary algorithms often get trapped in local optima, while expert knowledge can provide crucial guidance for accelerating convergence. Large language models (LLMs) offer powerful priors and reasoning ability, making them natural optimizers when expert knowledge matters. However, closed-source LLMs, though strong in exploration, cannot update their parameters and thus cannot internalize experience. Conversely, smaller open models can be continually fine-tuned but lack broad knowledge and reasoning strength. We introduce Multi-LLM Collaborative Co-evolution (MCCE), a hybrid framework that unites a frozen closed-source LLM with a lightweight trainable model. The system maintains a trajectory memory of past search processes; the small model is progressively refined via reinforcement learning, with the two models jointly supporting and complementing each other in global exploration. Unlike model distillation, this process enhances the capabilities of both models through mutual inspiration. Experiments on multi-objective drug design benchmarks show that MCCE achieves state-of-the-art Pareto front quality and consistently outperforms baselines. These results highlight a new paradigm for enabling continual evolution in hybrid LLM systems, combining knowledge-driven exploration with experience-driven learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Weidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaogeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-Chun","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Hung-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xusheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhi","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yinzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Harness is Not Secure: Benchmarking Real-world Threat of Command Line Interface Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:18:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T05:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Command-line interface (CLI) agents powered by large language models (LLMs) can interpret natural-language requests, plan multi-step tasks, execute shell commands, and modify files and system state. As these agents are increasingly used for operating-system (OS) workflows, it is important to evaluate whether they can be misused to carry out security-relevant operations. Existing benchmarks often lack an attacker-knowledge model grounded in tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), provide limited coverage of end-to-end kill chains, rely on simplified single-host environments, or use LLM-as-a-judge for success evaluation. We introduce AdvCLI, an MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK-aligned benchmark for evaluating OS-level misuse risks of CLI agents in a controlled multi-host sandbox. AdvCLI contains 140 tasks: 40 direct malicious requests, 74 TTP-based tasks, and 26 end-to-end kill chains. Each task is paired with deterministic hard-coded verification protocols that check whether the requested OS-level effect is realized. We evaluate seven CLI agents and products built on nine foundation models, including ReAct, OpenClaw, OpenAI Agent SDK, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor CLI, and Cursor IDE. Results show that current CLI agents frequently proceed beyond refusal and can complete a non-negligible fraction of malicious OS-level tasks, especially when requests include TTP-style attacker knowledge. AdvCLI provides a reproducible testbed for evaluating these risks and for developing stronger safety mechanisms for tool-using CLI agents in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:11Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:23:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhi","familyName":"Zang","name":"Zang, Hongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongji","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peihong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangzhi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Liangzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanlu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Quanlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLinf-VLA: A Unified and Efficient Framework for Reinforcement Learning of Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:05:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T10:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-12T08:36:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-13T00:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies have demonstrated the potential of reinforcement learning (RL) to improve the task performance of vision-language-action (VLA) models through interaction. However, current efforts remain fragmented, lacking a unified platform for fair comparison across architectures and algorithms, as well as an efficient system design for scalable training. Therefore, we present RLinf-VLA, a unified and efficient framework for scalable RL training of VLA models. RLinf-VLA standardizes the integration of diverse VLA architectures, RL algorithms, and heterogeneous simulators through a unified interface, enabling extensibility and reproducibility. To improve efficiency, the framework adopts a flexible resource allocation architecture for rendering, inference, and training in RL pipelines. In particular, RLinf-VLA introduces a hybrid fine-grained pipeline allocation strategy that achieves a 1.61$\\times$-1.88$\\times$ training speedup on ManiSkill. Using this framework, RL-trained models achieve strong performance across embodied benchmarks, including 98.11% success on 130 LIBERO tasks, 97.66% success on 25 ManiSkill tasks, and 84.63% average success across 6 RoboTwin tasks. In addition, RLinf-VLA distills a set of effective practices for RL-based VLA training. We envision RLinf-VLA as a foundational framework for efficient, unified, and reproducible research in embodied intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to RSS 2026. This is the technical report of the RLinf Team, focusing on the algorithm side. For the system-level design, please refer to arXiv:2509.15965. The open-sourced code link: https://github.com/RLinf/RLinf"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-13T02:11:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Bridge from Audio to Video: Phoneme-Viseme Alignment Allows Every Face to Speak Multiple Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:56:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-driven talking face synthesis (TFS) focuses on generating lifelike facial animations from speech input. Current TFS models perform well in English but struggle with non-English languages, producing inaccurate mouth shapes and rigid facial expressions. These limitations are mainly caused by English-dominated training datasets and the lack of cross-language generalization ability.To address these challenges, we propose Multilingual Experts (MuEx), a novel framework featuring a Phoneme-Guided Mixture-of-Experts (PG-MoE) architecture that employs phonemes and visemes as universal intermediaries to bridge the gap between audio and visual modalities, enabling lifelike multilingual TFS. We extract speech and visual features as phonemes and visemes, respectively, which represent the basic units of speech sounds and mouth movements, to alleviate linguistic differences and dataset bias.Furthermore, we introduce the Phoneme-Viseme Alignment Mechanism (PV-Align), which establishes robust cross-modal correspondences between phonemes and visemes to improve audiovisual synchronization. In addition, we construct a Multilingual Talking Face Dataset (MTFD) comprising 12 diverse languages with 95.04 hours of high-quality videos for training and evaluating multilingual TFS performance.Extensive experiments demonstrate that MuEx achieves superior performance across all languages in MTFD and exhibits effective zero-shot generalization to unseen languages without additional training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07152","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07152","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07152","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pihai","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Pihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiahang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DPL: Depth-only Perceptive Humanoid Locomotion via Realistic Depth Synthesis and Cross-Attention Terrain Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T05:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-03T09:26:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-04T01:44:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/lra.2026.3703584","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in legged robot perceptive locomotion have shown promising progress. However, terrain-aware humanoid locomotion remains largely constrained to two paradigms: depth image-based end-to-end learning and elevation map-based methods. The former suffers from limited training efficiency and a significant sim-to-real gap in depth perception, while the latter depends heavily on multiple vision sensors and localization systems, resulting in latency and reduced robustness. To overcome these challenges, we propose a novel framework that tightly integrates three key components: (1) Terrain-Aware Locomotion Policy with a Blind Backbone, which leverages pre-trained elevation map-based perception to guide reinforcement learning with minimal visual input; (2) Multi-Modality Cross-Attention Transformer, which reconstructs structured terrain representations from noisy depth images; (3) Realistic Depth Images Synthetic Method, which employs self-occlusion-aware ray casting and noise-aware modeling to synthesize realistic depth observations, achieving over 30\\% reduction in terrain reconstruction error. This combination enables efficient policy training with limited data and hardware resources, while preserving critical terrain features essential for generalization. We validate our framework on a full-sized humanoid robot, demonstrating agile and adaptive locomotion across diverse and challenging terrains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 8 figures. Published in IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters (RA-L), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07152","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fred","familyName":"Philippy","name":"Philippy, Fred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Bernardy","name":"Bernardy, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Siwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacques","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Jacques","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tegawendé F.","familyName":"Bissyandé","name":"Bissyandé, Tegawendé F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LuxInstruct: A Cross-Lingual Instruction Tuning Dataset For Luxembourgish"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:35:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-26T16:06:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:58:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction tuning has become a key technique for enhancing the performance of large language models, enabling them to better follow human prompts. However, low-resource languages such as Luxembourgish face severe limitations due to the lack of high-quality instruction datasets. Traditional reliance on machine translation often introduces semantic misalignment and cultural inaccuracies. In this work, we address these challenges by creating a cross-lingual instruction tuning dataset for Luxembourgish, without resorting to machine-generated translations into it. Instead, by leveraging aligned data from English, French, and German, we build a high-quality dataset that preserves linguistic and cultural nuances. We provide evidence that cross-lingual instruction tuning not only improves representational alignment across languages but also the model's generative capabilities in Luxembourgish. This highlights how cross-lingual data curation can avoid the common pitfalls of machine-translated data and directly benefit low-resource language development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper accepted at KONVENS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:15Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonggeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jonggeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Data Contamination in Psychometric Evaluations of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T06:29:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.findings-eacl.319","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies apply psychometric questionnaires to Large Language Models (LLMs) to assess high-level psychological constructs such as values, personality, moral foundations, and dark traits. Although prior work has raised concerns about possible data contamination from psychometric inventories, which may threaten the reliability of such evaluations, there has been no systematic attempt to quantify the extent of this contamination. To address this gap, we propose a framework to systematically measure data contamination in psychometric evaluations of LLMs, evaluating three aspects: (1) item memorization, (2) evaluation memorization, and (3) target score matching. Applying this framework to 21 models from major families and four widely used psychometric inventories, we provide evidence that popular inventories such as the Big Five Inventory (BFI-44) and Portrait Values Questionnaire (PVQ-40) exhibit strong contamination, where models not only memorize items but can also adjust their responses to achieve specific target scores."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:58:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Illing","name":"Illing, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Althoff","name":"Althoff, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SanDRA: Safe Large-Language-Model-Based Decision Making for Automated Vehicles Using Reachability Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-11T02:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:16:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been widely applied to knowledge-driven decision-making for automated vehicles due to their strong generalization and reasoning capabilities. However, the safety of the resulting decisions cannot be ensured due to possible hallucinations and the lack of integrated vehicle dynamics. To address this issue, we propose SanDRA, the first safe large-language-model-based decision making framework for automated vehicles using reachability analysis. Our approach starts with a comprehensive description of the driving scenario to prompt LLMs to generate and rank feasible driving actions. These actions are translated into temporal logic formulas that incorporate formalized traffic rules, and are subsequently integrated into reachability analysis to eliminate unsafe actions. We validate our approach in both open-loop and closed-loop driving environments using off-the-shelf and finetuned LLMs, showing that it can provide provably safe and, where possible, legally compliant driving actions, even under high-density traffic conditions. To ensure transparency and facilitate future research, all code and experimental setups are publicly available at github.com/CommonRoad/SanDRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"@2026 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Ghugare","name":"Ghugare, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger Creus","familyName":"Castanyer","name":"Castanyer, Roger Creus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathryn","familyName":"Wantlin","name":"Wantlin, Kathryn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Schofield","name":"Schofield, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Narasimhan","name":"Narasimhan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Eysenbach","name":"Eysenbach, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BuilderBench: The Building Blocks of Intelligent Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T02:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T13:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's AI models learn primarily through mimicry and refining, so it is not surprising that they struggle to solve problems beyond the limits set by existing data. To solve novel problems, agents should acquire skills by exploring and learning through experience. Finding a scalable learning mechanism for developing agents that learn through interaction remains a major open problem. In this work, we introduce BuilderBench, a benchmark to accelerate research into agent training that centers open-ended exploration. BuilderBench requires agents to learn how to build any structure using blocks. BuilderBench is equipped with (1) a simulator of a robot interacting with various physical blocks, and (2) a task-suite with over 50 diverse target structures that are carefully curated to test an understanding of physics, mathematics, and long-horizon planning. Agents are provided with a target structure at the start, and can interact with the environment for multiple episodes to experiment and learn various skills for building the structure. Solving these tasks requires \\emph{embodied reasoning} in a way that is not reflected in words but rather in actions, experimenting with different strategies and piecing them together. Our experiments with multiple state-of-the-art frontier language model based agents and tabula rasa reinforcement learning algorithms show that these agents cannot solve any of the non-trivial tasks in the BuilderBench. Our analysis throws light on the lack of exploration abilities in these models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Blogpost: https://rajghugare19.github.io/builderbench and Code: https://github.com/rajghugare19/builderbench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsheng","familyName":"Ba","name":"Ba, Jinsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sverrir","familyName":"Thorgeirsson","name":"Thorgeirsson, Sverrir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code Semantic Zooming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T09:42:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T19:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:02:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have introduced a new paradigm for software development, where source code is generated from natural language prompts. While this paradigm significantly boosts development productivity, building complex, real-world software systems remains challenging because natural language offers limited control over the code generation process. Inspired by the historical evolution of programming languages toward higher levels of abstraction, we advocate for a high-level abstraction language that gives developers greater control over LLM-assisted code writing. To this end, we propose Code Semantic Zooming (CodeZoom), a novel approach based on pseudocode that allows developers to iteratively explore, understand, and refine code across multiple layers of semantic abstraction. In a within-subjects user study (n=26), our method matches a state-of-the-art coding agent, Claude Code, on usability while producing a large effect on code comprehension: over 90% of participants reported feeling more in control of design decisions when using CodeZoom compared to using Claude Code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:11:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancheng","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Tiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLMs Reliable Rankers? Rank Manipulation via Two-Stage Token Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:40:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-28T18:43:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T01:13:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as rerankers in information retrieval, yet their ranking behavior can be steered by small, natural-sounding prompts. To expose this vulnerability, we present Rank Anything First (RAF), a two-stage token optimization method that crafts concise textual perturbations to consistently promote a target item in LLM-generated rankings while remaining hard to detect. Stage 1 uses Greedy Coordinate Gradient to shortlist candidate tokens at the current position by combining the gradient of the rank-target with a readability score; Stage 2 evaluates those candidates under exact ranking and readability losses using an entropy-based dynamic weighting scheme, and selects a token via temperature-controlled sampling. RAF generates ranking-promoting prompts token-by-token, guided by dual objectives: maximizing ranking effectiveness and preserving linguistic naturalness. Experiments across multiple LLMs show that RAF significantly boosts the rank of target items using naturalistic language, with greater robustness than existing methods in both promoting target items and maintaining naturalness. These findings underscore a critical security implication: LLM-based reranking is inherently susceptible to adversarial manipulation, raising new challenges for the trustworthiness and robustness of modern retrieval systems. Our code is available at: https://github.com/glad-lab/RAF."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06742","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06742","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06742","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Sarabadani","name":"Sarabadani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kheirolah Rahsepar","familyName":"Fard","name":"Fard, Kheirolah Rahsepar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MultiCNKG: Integrating Cognitive Neuroscience, Gene, and Disease Knowledge Graphs Using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T01:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.22034/tmi.2026.244943","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advent of large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized the integration of knowledge graphs (KGs) in biomedical and cognitive sciences, overcoming limitations in traditional machine learning methods for capturing intricate semantic links among genes, diseases, and cognitive processes. We introduce MultiCNKG, an innovative framework that merges three key knowledge sources: the Cognitive Neuroscience Knowledge Graph (CNKG) with 2.9K nodes and 4.3K edges across 9 node types and 20 edge types; Gene Ontology (GO) featuring 43K nodes and 75K edges in 3 node types and 4 edge types; and Disease Ontology (DO) comprising 11.2K nodes and 8.8K edges with 1 node type and 2 edge types. Leveraging LLMs like GPT-4, we conduct entity alignment, semantic similarity computation, and graph augmentation to create a cohesive KG that interconnects genetic mechanisms, neurological disorders, and cognitive functions. The resulting MultiCNKG encompasses 6.9K nodes across 5 types (e.g., Genes, Diseases, Cognitive Processes) and 11.3K edges spanning 7 types (e.g., Causes, Associated with, Regulates), facilitating a multi-layered view from molecular to behavioral domains. Assessments using metrics such as precision (85.20%), recall (87.30%), coverage (92.18%), graph consistency (82.50%), novelty detection (40.28%), and expert validation (89.50%) affirm its robustness and coherence. Link prediction evaluations with models like TransE (MR: 391, MRR: 0.411) and RotatE (MR: 263, MRR: 0.395) show competitive performance against benchmarks like FB15k-237 and WN18RR. This KG advances applications in personalized medicine, cognitive disorder diagnostics, and hypothesis formulation in cognitive neuroscience."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06742","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:35:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Shahriar","name":"Shahriar, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nafiu","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Nafiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Sadif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farig","familyName":"Sadeque","name":"Sadeque, Farig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rizwan","familyName":"Parvez","name":"Parvez, Md Rizwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Agentic Security: Applications, Threats and Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-20T13:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T21:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are now used throughout cybersecurity. While these agents facilitate powerful and autonomous security applications, their autonomy opens up new attack surfaces, and the security community is actively building defenses to secure them. Yet the literature on this subject has grown quickly and unevenly. Existing surveys treat applications, threats, and defenses in isolation, leaving no unified account of how an agent's capabilities, vulnerabilities, and countermeasures interconnect. In this work we present the first holistic survey of the agentic security landscape, structuring the field around the fundamental pillars of Applications, Threats and Defenses. We provide a comprehensive taxonomy of over 260 papers, explaining how agents are used in downstream cybersecurity applications, inherent threats to agentic systems, and countermeasures designed to protect them. In addition, we provide detailed pillar-specific and cross-cutting analyses that show the security-lifecycle coverage of agentic applications, comparison between red-teaming and blue-teaming agents, and the adversarial use of red-teaming applications. On the threat side, we analyze the entry points and agent-loop stages that attacks target, their specificity to the agentic setting, and the threat models they assume. On the defense side, we analyze the prevailing defense strategies, their cost and security trade-offs, and where in the agent lifecycle they are deployed. We further map which defenses cover which attack classes and chart trends in agent architecture, backbone model usage, data modality coverage, and the growth of attack and defense research over time. Taken together, these findings indicate that agentic systems are structurally fragile by default and that securing them will require defenses that span the full agent lifecycle rather than single-layer fixes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:27Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subin","familyName":"An","name":"An, Subin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yugyeong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yugyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heejin","familyName":"Kook","name":"Kook, Heejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josh","familyName":"Seltzer","name":"Seltzer, Josh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transparent Reference-free Automated Evaluation of Open-Ended User Survey Responses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:37:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:05:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-industry.65","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-ended survey responses provide valuable insights in marketing research, but low-quality responses not only burden researchers with manual filtering but also risk leading to misleading conclusions, underscoring the need for effective evaluation. Existing automatic evaluation methods target LLM-generated text and inadequately assess human-written responses with their distinct characteristics. To address such characteristics, we propose a two-stage evaluation framework specifically designed for human survey responses. First, gibberish filtering removes nonsensical responses. Then, three dimensions-effort, relevance, and completeness-are evaluated using LLM capabilities, grounded in empirical analysis of real-world survey data. Validation on English and Korean datasets shows that our framework not only outperforms existing metrics but also demonstrates high practical applicability for real-world applications such as response quality prediction and response rejection, showing strong correlations with expert assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Industry Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:43Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07061","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07061","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07061","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Hossein","familyName":"Yari","name":"Yari, Amir Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kalmit","familyName":"Kulkarni","name":"Kulkarni, Kalmit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad Raza","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Ahmad Raza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fajri","familyName":"Koto","name":"Koto, Fajri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Metric Reliability for Fine-grained Evaluation of Machine Translation and Summarization in Indian Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-09T08:32:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:38:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While automatic metrics drive progress in Machine Translation (MT) and Text Summarization (TS), existing metrics have been developed and validated almost exclusively for English and other high-resource languages. This narrow focus leaves Indian languages, spoken by over 1.5 billion people, largely overlooked, casting doubt on the universality of current evaluation practices. To address this gap, we introduce ITEM, a large-scale benchmark that systematically evaluates the alignment of 29 automatic metrics with human judgments across six major Indian languages, enriched with fine-grained annotations. Our extensive evaluation, covering agreement with human judgments, sensitivity to outliers, language-specific reliability, inter-metric correlations, and resilience to controlled perturbations reveals four central findings: (1) LLM-based evaluators show the strongest alignment with human judgments at both segment and system levels; (2) outliers exert a significant impact on metric-human agreement; (3) In TS, metrics are more effective at capturing content fidelity, whereas in MT, they better reflect fluency; and (4) Metrics differ in their robustness and sensitivity when subjected to diverse perturbations. Collectively, these findings offer critical guidance for advancing metric design and evaluation in Indian languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07061","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07315","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07315","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07315","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting-Yun","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ting-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qingze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiance","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Xiance","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Garrette","name":"Garrette, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shyam","familyName":"Upadhyay","name":"Upadhyay, Shyam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremiah","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jeremiah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Schillings","name":"Schillings, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SWE-IF: Aligning Code Evaluation with Human Preference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:30:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:14:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have catalyzed vibe coding, where users leverage LLMs to generate and iteratively refine code through natural language interactions until it passes their vibe check. Vibe check reflects human preference and goes beyond functionality: the solution should feel right, read cleanly, preserve intent, and remain correct. However, current code evaluation remains anchored to pass@k and captures only functional correctness, overlooking non-functional instructions that users routinely apply. In this paper, we hypothesize that instruction following is the missing piece underlying vibe check besides functional correctness. To quantify models' code instruction-following capabilities with measurable signals, we present VeriCode, a taxonomy of 30 verifiable code instructions together with deterministic verifiers. We use the taxonomy to augment established evaluation suites, resulting in SWE-IF, a testbed to assess both instruction following and functional correctness. Evaluating 31 LLMs, we show that even the strongest models struggle to comply with multiple instructions and exhibit functional regression. Most importantly, a composite score of functional correctness and instruction following correlates best with human preference, with instruction following emerging as the primary differentiator among LLMs. Our code, data, and taxonomy are available at https://github.com/maszhongming/SWE-IF."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07315","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim El","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Karim El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Zanella","name":"Zanella, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"De Vleeschouwer","name":"De Vleeschouwer, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Macq","name":"Macq, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few-Shot Adaptation Benchmark for Remote Sensing Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:29:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:05:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Remote Sensing Vision-Language Models (RSVLMs) have shown remarkable potential thanks to large-scale pretraining, achieving strong zero-shot performance on various tasks. However, their ability to generalize in low-data regimes, such as few-shot learning, remains insufficiently explored. In this work, we present the first structured benchmark for evaluating few-shot adaptation methods on RSVLMs. We conduct comprehensive experiments across ten remote sensing scene classification datasets, applying five widely used few-shot adaptation strategies to three state-of-the-art RSVLMs with varying backbones. Our findings reveal that models with similar zero-shot performance can exhibit markedly different behavior under few-shot adaptation, with some RSVLMs being inherently more amenable to such adaptation than others. The variability of performance and the absence of a clear winner among existing methods highlight the need for the development of more robust methods for few-shot adaptation tailored to RS. To facilitate future research, we provide a reproducible benchmarking framework and open-source code to systematically evaluate RSVLMs under few-shot conditions. The source code is publicly available on Github: https://github.com/elkhouryk/fewshot_RSVLMs"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengxiao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Mengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Pierri","name":"Pierri, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing Social Identity Bias in Chinese LLMs with Gendered Pronouns and Social Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:00:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T21:29:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:15:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in user-facing applications, raising concerns that they may reflect and amplify social biases. We investigate social identity biases in Chinese LLMs using Mandarin-specific prompts across ten representative models. Our evaluation compares ingroup (\"We\") and outgroup (\"They\") framings across 240 social groups salient in the Chinese context, using a two-tiered measurement framework that assesses both sentiment and toxicity. The prompt design explicitly accounts for linguistic properties of Mandarin, including the distinction between the default gender-neutral plural pronoun and its explicitly feminine counterpart, enabling a controlled comparison of social identity framing effects. Across models, we observe systematic ingroup-outgroup asymmetries, although their expression differs across measurement dimensions. In particular, instruction tuning often reduces sentiment asymmetries, while toxicity gaps remain more persistent. Moreover, the feminine-marked plural pronoun is associated with higher toxicity than the default gender-neutral plural in several models. Our study introduces a language-aware evaluation framework for Chinese LLMs and shows that (i) social identity biases previously documented in English also manifest in Chinese and that (ii) Mandarin-specific linguistic structure can reveal bias patterns that are not directly observable in English-only settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:50Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanyun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:10:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:58:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive capabilities across diverse application domains. Recent work has explored Multi-LLM Agent Debate (MAD) as a way to enhance performance by enabling multiple LLMs to discuss and refine responses iteratively. Nevertheless, existing MAD methods predominantly focus on utilizing external structures, such as debate graphs, using LLM-as-a-Judge, while neglecting the application of self signals, such as token logits and attention, that arise during generation. This omission leads to redundant computation and potential performance degradation. In this paper, we shift the focus to the self signals of multi-LLM debate and introduce a Self-Signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate (SID), which leverages two types of self-signals: model-level confidence and token-level semantic focus, to adaptively guide the debate process. Our approach enables high-confidence agents to exit early at the model level and compress the redundant debate contents based on the attention mechanism. We evaluate our method on various LLMs and Multimodal LLMs across multiple challenging benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that our method not only outperforms existing MAD techniques in accuracy but also reduces token consumption, highlighting the effectiveness of utilizing self signals in enhancing both the performance and efficiency of multi-agent debate systems. Our code will be available at~\\href{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}{\\texttt{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udbhav","familyName":"Bamba","name":"Bamba, Udbhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XRPO: Pushing the limits of GRPO with Targeted Exploration and Exploitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T01:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:20:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning algorithms such as GRPO have driven recent advances in large language model (LLM) reasoning. While scaling the number of rollouts stabilizes training, existing approaches suffer from limited exploration on challenging prompts and leave informative feedback signals underexploited, due to context-independent rollout allocation across prompts (e.g., generating 16 rollouts per prompt) and relying heavily on sparse rewards. This paper presents XRPO(eXplore - eXploit GRPO), a unified framework that recasts policy optimization through the principled lens of rollout exploration-exploitation. To enhance exploration, XRPO introduces a mathematically grounded rollout allocator that adaptively prioritizes prompts with higher potential for uncertainty reduction. It further addresses stagnation on zero-reward prompts through an in-context seeding strategy that injects curated exemplars, steering the model into more difficult reasoning trajectories. To strengthen exploitation, XRPO develops a group-relative, novelty-aware advantage sharpening mechanism that leverages sequence likelihoods to amplify low-probability yet correct responses, thereby extending the policy's reach beyond sparse rewards. Experiments across diverse math and coding benchmarks on both reasoning and non-reasoning models demonstrate that XRPO outperforms existing advances (e.g., GRPO and GSPO) up to 4% pass@1 and 6% cons@32, while accelerating training convergence by up to 2.7X."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Kreitner","name":"Kreitner, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Hager","name":"Hager, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Mengedoht","name":"Mengedoht, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Kaissis","name":"Kaissis, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Rueckert","name":"Rueckert, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin J.","familyName":"Menten","name":"Menten, Martin J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient numeracy in language models through single-token number embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:48:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T14:19:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To drive progress in science and engineering, large language models (LLMs) must be able to process large amounts of numerical data and solve long calculations efficiently. This is currently only possible through the use of external tools or extensive reasoning chains, either weakening the numerical representations of LLMs or limiting the length of problems they can solve. We show that frontier LLMs require excessive amounts of reasoning tokens to solve even basic calculations, which is exacerbated by their tokenization strategies that split single numbers into multiple tokens. This motivates the need for efficient and effective single-token number encodings. We introduce a set of desiderata for such encodings and show that existing approaches fail to fulfill them. To address these shortcomings, we propose BitTokens, a novel encoding strategy that represents any number as a single token using its IEEE 754 binary floating-point representation. Through extensive experiments we show that our BitTokens allow even small language models to learn algorithms that solve basic arithmetic operations nearly perfectly. This newly gained efficiency could expand the length and complexity of problems language models can solve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Ziakas","name":"Ziakas, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Loo","name":"Loo, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishita","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Nishita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandra","familyName":"Russo","name":"Russo, Alessandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Red-Bandit: Test-Time Adaptation for LLM Red-Teaming via Bandit-Guided LoRA Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:06:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-17T13:01:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:09:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated red-teaming has emerged as a scalable approach for auditing Large Language Models (LLMs) prior to deployment, yet existing approaches lack mechanisms to efficiently adapt to model-specific vulnerabilities at inference. We introduce Red-Bandit, a red-teaming framework that adapts online to identify and exploit model failure modes under distinct attack styles (e.g., manipulation, slang). Red-Bandit post-trains a set of parameter-efficient LoRA experts, each specialized for a particular attack style, using reinforcement learning that rewards the generation of unsafe prompts via a rule-based safety model. At inference, a multi-armed bandit policy dynamically selects among these attack-style experts based on the target model's response safety, balancing exploration and exploitation. Red-Bandit achieves state-of-the-art results on AdvBench under sufficient exploration (ASR@10), while producing more human-readable prompts (lower perplexity). Moreover, Red-Bandit's bandit policy serves as a diagnostic tool for uncovering model-specific vulnerabilities by indicating which attack styles most effectively elicit unsafe behaviors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the Main Conference at ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenguo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhenguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujiao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yujiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiji","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Shiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Gao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VA-Adapter: Adapting Ultrasound Foundation Model to Echocardiography Probe Guidance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:38:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T04:42:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:39:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-18T15:55:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T02:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Echocardiography is a critical tool for detecting heart diseases, yet its steep operational difficulty causes a shortage of skilled personnel. Probe guidance systems, which assist in acquiring high-quality images, offer a promising solution to lower this operational barrier. However, robust probe guidance remains challenging due to significant individual variability. This variability manifests as differences in low-level features within two-dimensional (2D) images, which complicates image feature understanding, and differences in individual three-dimensional (3D) structures, which poses challenges for precise navigation. To address these challenges, we first propose leveraging the robust image representations learned by ultrasound foundation models from vast datasets. Yet, applying these models to probe navigation is non-trivial due to their lack of understanding of individual 3D structures. To this end, we meticulously design a Vision-Action Adapter (VA-Adapter) to online inject the capability of understanding individual 3D structures. Specifically, by embedding the VA-Adapter into the foundation model's image encoder, the model can infer cardiac anatomy from historical vision-action sequences, mimicking the cognitive process of a sonographer. Extensive experiments on a dataset with over 1.31M samples demonstrate that the VA-Adapter outperforms strong probe guidance models while requiring approximately 33 times fewer trained parameters. Code is available at https://github.com/LeapLabTHU/VA-Adapter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MICCAI2026 Early Accept Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Nagaraj","name":"Nagaraj, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sakshi","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Sakshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utkarsh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Utkarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak","familyName":"Ravikumar","name":"Ravikumar, Deepak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRIM: Token-wise Attention-Derived Saliency for Data-Efficient Instruction Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:11:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T14:13:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:49:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T16:54:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T01:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction tuning is essential for aligning large language models (LLMs) to downstream tasks and commonly relies on large, diverse corpora. However, small, high-quality subsets, known as coresets, can deliver comparable or superior results, though curating them remains challenging. Existing methods often rely on coarse, sample-level signals like gradients, an approach that is computationally expensive and overlooks fine-grained features. To address this, we introduce TRIM (Token Relevance via Interpretable Multi-layer Attention), a forward-only, token-centric framework. Instead of using gradients, TRIM operates by matching underlying representational patterns identified via attention-based \"fingerprints\" from a handful of target samples. Such an approach makes TRIM highly efficient and uniquely sensitive to the structural features that define a task. Coresets selected by our method consistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines by up to 9% on downstream tasks and even surpass the performance of full-data fine-tuning in some settings. By avoiding expensive backward passes, TRIM achieves this at a fraction of the computational cost. These findings establish TRIM as a scalable and efficient alternative for building high-quality instruction-tuning datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junki","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Junki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Kakizaki","name":"Kakizaki, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiki","familyName":"Miyagawa","name":"Miyagawa, Taiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentially Private Synthetic Text Generation for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:15:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T15:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) by grounding them in external knowledge. However, its application in sensitive domains is limited by privacy risks. Existing private RAG methods typically rely on query-time differential privacy (DP), which requires repeated noise injection and leads to accumulated privacy loss. To address this issue, we propose DP-SynRAG, a framework that uses LLMs to generate differentially private synthetic RAG databases. Unlike prior methods, the synthetic text can be reused once created, thereby avoiding repeated noise injection and additional privacy costs. To preserve essential information for downstream RAG tasks, DP-SynRAG extends private prediction, which instructs LLMs to generate text that mimics subsampled database records in a DP manner. Experiments show that DP-SynRAG achieves superior performance to the state-of-the-art private RAG systems while maintaining a fixed privacy budget, offering a scalable solution for privacy-preserving RAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firoj","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Firoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Ezzat","familyName":"Shahroor","name":"Shahroor, Ali Ezzat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Arid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Arid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zien Sheikh","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Zien Sheikh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hunzalah Hassan","familyName":"Bhatti","name":"Bhatti, Hunzalah Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Bayan","familyName":"Kmainasi","name":"Kmainasi, Mohamed Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shammur Absar","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Shammur Absar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basel","familyName":"Mousi","name":"Mousi, Basel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahim","familyName":"Dalvi","name":"Dalvi, Fahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadir","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Nadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasa","familyName":"Milic-Frayling","name":"Milic-Frayling, Natasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OASIS: A Multilingual and Multimodal Dataset for Culturally Grounded Spoken Visual QA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T06:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:32:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T05:22:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:35:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale multimodal models achieve strong results on tasks like Visual Question Answering (VQA), but they are often limited when queries require cultural and visual information, everyday knowledge, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented languages. We introduce OASIS, a large-scale culturally grounded multimodal QA dataset covering images, text, and speech. OASIS is built with EverydayMMQA, a scalable semi-automatic framework for creating localized spoken and visual QA resources, supported by multi-stage human-in-the-loop validation. OASIS contains approximately 0.92M real images and 14.8M QA pairs, including 3.7M spoken questions, with 383 hours of human-recorded speech, and 20K hours of voice-cloned speech, from 42 speakers. It supports four input settings: text-only, speech-only, text+image, and speech+image. The dataset focuses on English and Arabic varieties across 18 countries, covering Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) as well as dialectal Arabic. It is designed to evaluate models beyond object recognition, targeting pragmatic, commonsense, and culturally grounded reasoning in real-world scenarios. We benchmark four closed-source models, three open-source models, and one fine-tuned model on OASIS. The framework and dataset will be made publicly available to the community. https://huggingface.co/datasets/QCRI/OASIS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Multimodal Foundation Models, Large Language Models, Native, Multilingual, Language Diversity, Contextual Understanding, Culturally Informed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajvee","familyName":"Sheth","name":"Sheth, Rajvee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samridhi Raj","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Samridhi Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahavir","familyName":"Patil","name":"Patil, Mahavir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Beniwal","name":"Beniwal, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Monolingual Assumptions: A Survey of Code-Switched NLP in the Era of Large Language Models across Modalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T04:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T11:58:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:47:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-07T16:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:53:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-08T04:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-21T04:18:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:31:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Amidst the rapid advances of large language models (LLMs), most LLMs still struggle with mixed-language inputs, limited Codeswitching (CSW) datasets, and evaluation biases, which hinder their deployment in multilingual societies. This survey provides the first comprehensive analysis of CSW-aware LLM research, reviewing 327 studies spanning five research areas, 15+ NLP tasks, 30+ datasets, and 80+ languages. We categorize recent advances by architecture, training strategy, and evaluation methodology, outlining how LLMs have reshaped CSW modeling and identifying the challenges that persist. The paper concludes with a roadmap that emphasizes the need for inclusive datasets, fair evaluation, and linguistically grounded models to achieve truly multilingual capabilities https://github.com/lingo-iitgn/awesome-code-mixing/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:21Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olayiwola","familyName":"Arowolo","name":"Arowolo, Olayiwola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jochen L.","familyName":"Cremer","name":"Cremer, Jochen L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Generalization of Graph Neural Networks for AC Optimal Power Flow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:28:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:39:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T10:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:47:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AC Optimal Power Flow (ACOPF) is computationally intensive for large-scale grids, often requiring prohibitive solution times with conventional solvers. Machine learning offers significant speedups, but existing models struggle with scalability and topology flexibility. To address these challenges, we propose a Hybrid Heterogeneous Message Passing Neural Network (HH-MPNN) that integrates a heterogeneous graph neural network (GNN) with a scalable transformer and physics-informed positional encodings. Our architecture explicitly models distinct power system components to capture local features while using global attention for long-range dependencies. Evaluated on diverse benchmarks, including PGLearn and GridFM-DataKit datasets, HH-MPNN achieves less than 1% optimality gap on default topologies across grid sizes from 14 to 2,000 buses. For N-1 contingencies, our approach demonstrates zero-shot N-1 generalization with less than 3% optimality gap on several test cases despite training only on default topologies. We further develop an approach that ensures robust N-1 generalization to high-impact contingencies through targeted augmentation of the training data, showing that exhaustive simulation is unnecessary for topologically flexible models. Finally, size generalization experiments demonstrate that pre-training on small grids significantly improves performance on large-scale systems. Achieving computational speedups of up to 5,000 times compared to interior point solvers, these results advance practical, generalizable machine learning for real-time power system operations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Pre-print has been submitted for review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonggeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jonggeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haesung","familyName":"Pyun","name":"Pyun, Haesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Adapt Small Language Models for Tools; Adapt Tool Schemas to the Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T09:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-19T15:11:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs) enable scalable tool-augmented multi-agent systems where multiple SLMs handle subtasks orchestrated by a powerful coordinator. However, they struggle with tool-use tasks, particularly in selecting appropriate tools and identifying correct parameters. A common failure mode is \\textit{schema misalignment}: models hallucinate plausible tool names that are absent from the provided tool schema, due to different naming conventions internalized during pretraining. Rather than training models to adapt to unfamiliar schemas, we propose adapting schemas to align with models' pretrained knowledge. We introduce \\textbf{PA-Tool} (Pretraining-Aligned Tool Schema Generation), a training-free method that leverages peakedness, a signal used in contamination detection that indicates pretraining familiarity, to rename tool components. By generating multiple candidates and selecting the candidate with the highest peakedness, PA-Tool identifies pretraining-aligned naming patterns. Experiments on MetaTool and RoTBench show improvements of up to 17\\%, with schema misalignment errors reduced by 80\\%. PA-Tool enables small models to substantially improve tool-use accuracy without retraining, showing that schema-level interventions can unlock the tool-use potential of resource-efficient models. Our code is available at https://github.com/holi-lab/PA-Tool."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wensong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Haocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lutao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are We Using the Right Benchmark: An Evaluation Framework for Visual Token Compression Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T06:35:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts to accelerate inference in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have largely focused on visual token compression. The effectiveness of these methods is commonly evaluated by measuring the accuracy drop on existing MLLM benchmarks before and after compression. However, these benchmarks are originally designed to assess general perception and reasoning abilities, rather than the specific challenges posed by visual token compression, leading to a fundamental task mismatch. In this work, we uncover a counterintuitive yet consistent phenomenon: simple image downsampling outperforms many advanced visual token compression methods across multiple widely used benchmarks. Through a comprehensive empirical study spanning eight popular benchmarks and multiple state-of-the-art compression techniques, we show that (i) current benchmarks contain substantial noise (task-irrelevant samples) for evaluating visual token compression, and (ii) downsampling can act as an effective data filter that distinguishes between simple and difficult samples with respect to compression sensitivity. Motivated by these findings, we propose VTC-Bench, an evaluation framework that explicitly leverages downsampling as a discriminator to denoise existing benchmarks, enabling a fairer and more meaningful additional assessment of visual token compression methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL2026 Main. Code: https://github.com/Chenfei-Liao/VTC-Bench; Project Page: https://chenfei-liao.github.io/VTC-Bench-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Bertolazzi","name":"Bertolazzi, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandro","familyName":"Pezzelle","name":"Pezzelle, Sandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raffaella","familyName":"Bernardi","name":"Bernardi, Raffaella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Language Models Conflate Logical Validity with Plausibility: A Representational Analysis of Content Effects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:48:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T09:31:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:21:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Both humans and large language models (LLMs) exhibit content effects: biases in which the plausibility of the semantic content of a reasoning problem influences judgments regarding its logical validity. While this phenomenon in humans is best explained by the dual-process theory of reasoning, the mechanisms behind content effects in LLMs remain unclear. In this work, we address this issue by investigating how LLMs encode the concepts of validity and plausibility within their internal representations. We show that both concepts are linearly represented and strongly aligned in representational geometry, leading models to conflate plausibility with validity. Using steering vectors, we demonstrate that plausibility vectors can causally bias validity judgements, and vice versa, and that the degree of alignment between these two concepts predicts the magnitude of behavioral content effects across models. Finally, we construct debiasing vectors that disentangle these concepts, reducing content effects and improving reasoning accuracy. Our findings advance understanding of how abstract logical concepts are represented in LLMs and highlight representational interventions as a path toward more logical systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfei","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengdi","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Fengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriEquivBench: An Equivalence Score for Ground-Truth-Free Evaluation of Formally Verifiable Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:19:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T09:53:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:47:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-18T14:30:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:45:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Formal verification is the next frontier for ensuring the correctness of code generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). While methods that co-generate code and formal specifications in formal languages, like Dafny, can, in principle, prove alignment with user intent, progress is bottlenecked by specification quality evaluation. Current benchmarks rely on matching against ground-truth specifications, a manual and expertise-intensive process that has limited existing datasets to a few hundred simple problems and also suffers from a reliability issue. To address this, we introduce VeriEquivBench, a new benchmark with $2,389$ complex algorithmic problems that probe the limitations of current models in both code generation and formal reasoning. Our evaluation framework replaces ground-truth matching with a formally grounded metric, the equivalence score, and rigorously verifies the quality of generated specifications and code. Our results show that generating formally verifiable code remains a profound challenge for state-of-the-art LLMs. This underscores both the difficulty of the task and the need for benchmarks like VeriEquivBench to drive progress toward scalable and reliable coding agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting-Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ting-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raviteja","familyName":"Vemulapalli","name":"Vemulapalli, Raviteja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hema Swetha","familyName":"Koppula","name":"Koppula, Hema Swetha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Cemri","name":"Cemri, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COMPASS: Benchmarking Constrained Optimization in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:09:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T19:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human decision-making often involves constrained optimization. As LLM agents are deployed to assist with real-world tasks like travel planning, shopping, and scheduling, they must mirror this capability. We introduce COMPASS, a benchmark that evaluates whether LLM agents can perform constrained optimization in realistic travel planning settings. To success in these tasks, agents must engage in multi-turn conversations with user to gather task information as well as use tools to gather information from the database. Then agents must propose a solution that not only satisfies hard constraints but also optimizes user's utility objective. Evaluating state-of-the-art models, we reveal a significant feasible-optimal gap: while models achieve 70-90% feasibility (constraint satisfaction), they reach only 20-60% optimality (utility optimization). Our analysis shows that tool use is not the bottleneck. Instead, the core limitation is insufficient exploration of the search space, with success strongly correlating with information gathered. Coding agents show a promising approach to mitigate this gap. Together, COMPASS provides a testbed for developing LLM agents that can truly mirror human decision-making by both satisfying constraints and optimizing objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minju","familyName":"Gwak","name":"Gwak, Minju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guijin","familyName":"Son","name":"Son, Guijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting the Uniform Information Density Hypothesis in LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:37:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T03:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T04:43:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:25:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Uniform Information Density (UID) hypothesis proposes that effective communication is achieved by maintaining a stable flow of information. In this work, we revisit this principle in the context of Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning, asking whether step-level uniformity reflects reasoning quality. To this end, we introduce a novel framework to quantify uniformity of information flow at both local and global levels, using an entropy-based stepwise density metric. Across experiments on seven reasoning benchmarks, we see a counter-intuitive pattern: while high-quality reasoning exhibit smooth step-by-step transitions local uniformity and structured, non-uniform information flow at the trajectory level global non-uniformity. The results demonstrate that these uniformities outperform alternative internal signals as predictors of reasoning quality, and such divergence with human communication is not a model deficiency, but a byproduct of distinct objectives between human communication and LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksi","familyName":"Huotala","name":"Huotala, Aleksi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miikka","familyName":"Kuutila","name":"Kuutila, Miikka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olli-Pekka","familyName":"Turtio","name":"Turtio, Olli-Pekka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simo","familyName":"Sipilä","name":"Sipilä, Simo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mika","familyName":"Mäntylä","name":"Mäntylä, Mika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AISysRev -- LLM-based Tool for Title-abstract Screening"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:59:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T12:02:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T05:08:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conducting systematic reviews is laborious. In the screening or study selection phase, the number of papers can be overwhelming. Recent research has demonstrated that large language models (LLMs) can perform title-abstract screening and support humans in the task. To this end, we developed AISysRev, an LLM-based screening tool implemented as a containerized web application. The tool accepts CSV files containing paper titles and abstracts. Users specify inclusion and exclusion criteria. Multiple different LLMs can be used, such as Gemini, Claude, Mistral or ChatGPT via OpenRouter. We also support locally hosted models and any model compatible with the OpenAI SDK. AISysRev implements both zero-shot and few-shot prompting, and also allows for manual screening through interfaces that display LLM results as guidance for human reviewers. LLM calls are parallelized, meaning screening speed is typically between 100 to 300 papers per minute, depending on the model and the host. To demonstrate the tool's use in practice, we conducted a qualitative trial study with 137 papers using the tool. Our findings indicate that papers can be classified into four categories: Easy Includes, Easy Excludes, Boundary Includes, and Boundary Excludes. The Boundary cases, where LLMs are prone to errors, highlight the need for human intervention. While LLMs do not replace human judgment in systematic reviews, they can reduce the burden of assessing large volumes of scientific literature.\n Video: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HeblemlgnAQ Tool: https://github.com/EvoTestOps/AISysRev"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages + references. Accepted for publication in the Companion Proceedings of the 34th ACM Symposium on the Foundations of Software Engineering (FSE '26), June 5--9, 2026, Montreal, Canada"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jusen","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weigao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weigao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Native Hybrid Attention for Efficient Sequence Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T13:24:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers excel at sequence modeling but face quadratic complexity, while linear attention offers improved efficiency but often compromises recall accuracy over long contexts. In this work, we introduce Native Hybrid Attention (NHA), a novel hybrid architecture of linear and full attention that integrates both intra \u0026amp; inter-layer hybridization into a unified layer design. NHA maintains long-term context in key-value slots updated by a linear RNN, and augments them with short-term tokens from a sliding window. A single softmax attention operation is then applied over all keys and values, enabling per-token and per-head context-dependent weighting without requiring additional fusion parameters. The inter-layer behavior is controlled through a single hyperparameter, the sliding window size, which allows smooth adjustment between purely linear and full attention while keeping all layers structurally uniform. Experimental results show that NHA surpasses Transformers and other hybrid baselines on recall-intensive and commonsense reasoning tasks. Furthermore, pretrained LLMs can be structurally hybridized with NHA, achieving competitive accuracy while delivering significant efficiency gains. Code is available at https://github.com/JusenD/NHA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL 2026, 17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Pengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingwen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Qingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human-aligned AI Model Cards with Weighted Hierarchy Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T11:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:33:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T08:06:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:35:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3803437.3805265","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to a burgeoning ecosystem of specialized, domain-specific models. While this rapid growth accelerates innovation, it has simultaneously created significant challenges in model discovery and adoption. Users struggle to navigate this landscape due to inconsistent, incomplete, and imbalanced documentation across platforms. Existing documentation frameworks, such as Model Cards and FactSheets, attempt to standardize reporting but are often static, predominantly qualitative, and lack the quantitative mechanisms needed for rigorous cross-model comparison. This gap exacerbates model underutilization and hinders responsible adoption. To address these shortcomings, we introduce the Comprehensive Responsible AI Model Card Framework (CRAI-MCF), a novel approach that transitions from static disclosures to actionable, human-aligned documentation. Grounded in Value Sensitive Design (VSD), CRAI-MCF is built upon an empirical analysis of 240 open-source projects, distilling 217 parameters into an eight-module, value-aligned architecture. Our framework introduces a quantitative sufficiency criterion to operationalize evaluation and enables rigorous cross-model comparison under a unified scheme. By balancing technical, ethical, and operational dimensions, CRAI-MCF empowers practitioners to efficiently assess, select, and adopt LLMs with greater confidence and operational integrity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures, 9 tables. Published in FSE Companion 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:12Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jigang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Jigang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoran","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Xiaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengdong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shengdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanming","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weian","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Weian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evolutionary Profiles for Protein Fitness Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:46:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T18:18:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-11T09:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:31:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting the fitness impact of mutations is central to protein engineering but constrained by limited assays relative to the size of sequence space. Protein language models (pLMs) trained with masked language modeling (MLM) exhibit strong zero-shot fitness prediction; we provide a unifying view by interpreting natural evolution as implicit reward maximization and MLM as inverse reinforcement learning (IRL), in which extant sequences act as expert demonstrations and pLM log-odds serve as fitness estimates. Building on this perspective, we introduce EvoIF, a lightweight model that integrates two complementary sources of evolutionary signal: (i) within-family profiles from retrieved homologs and (ii) cross-family structural-evolutionary constraints distilled from inverse folding logits. EvoIF fuses sequence-structure representations with these profiles via a compact transition block, yielding calibrated probabilities for log-odds scoring. On ProteinGym (217 mutational assays; \u0026gt;2.5M mutants), EvoIF and its MSA-enabled variant achieve state-of-the-art or competitive performance while using only 0.15% of the training data and fewer parameters than recent large models. Ablations confirm that within-family and cross-family profiles are complementary, improving robustness across function types, MSA depths, taxa, and mutation depths. The codes will be made publicly available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan R.","familyName":"Christ","name":"Christ, Bryan R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penelope","familyName":"Molitz","name":"Molitz, Penelope","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beau","familyName":"LeBlond","name":"LeBlond, Beau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Gottesman","name":"Gottesman, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Kropko","name":"Kropko, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hartvigsen","name":"Hartvigsen, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EDUMATH: Generating Standards-aligned Educational Math Word Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:53:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T21:29:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:47:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Math word problems (MWPs) are critical K-12 educational tools, and customizing them to students' interests and ability levels can enhance learning. However, teachers struggle to find time to customize MWPs for students given large class sizes and increasing burnout. We propose that LLMs can support math education by generating MWPs customized to student interests and math education standards. We use a joint human expert-LLM judge approach to evaluate over 11,000 MWPs generated by open and closed LLMs and develop the first teacher-annotated dataset for standards-aligned educational MWP generation. We show the value of our data by using it to train a 12B open model that matches the performance of larger and more capable open models. We also use our teacher-annotated data to train a text classifier that enables a 30B open LLM to outperform existing closed baselines without any training. Next, we show our models' MWPs are more similar to human-written MWPs than those from existing models. We conclude by conducting the first study of customized LLM-generated MWPs with grade school students, finding they perform similarly on our models' MWPs relative to human-written MWPs but consistently prefer our customized MWPs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 16 figures ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepeng","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Zhepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jielin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Webscale-RL: Automated Data Pipeline for Scaling RL Data to Pretraining Levels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T22:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:11:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-10T04:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-13T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success through imitation learning on vast text corpora, but this paradigm creates a training-generation gap and limits robust reasoning. Reinforcement learning (RL) offers a more data-efficient solution capable of bridging this gap, yet its application has been constrained by a critical data bottleneck: existing RL datasets are orders of magnitude smaller and less diverse than web-scale pre-training corpora. To address this, we introduce the Webscale-RL pipeline, a scalable data engine that systematically converts large-scale pre-training documents into millions of diverse, verifiable question-answer pairs for RL. Using this pipeline, we construct the Webscale-RL dataset, containing 1.2 million examples across more than 9 domains. Our experiments show that the model trained on this dataset significantly outperforms continual pretraining and strong data refinement baselines across a suite of benchmarks. Notably, RL training with our dataset proves substantially more efficient, achieving the performance of continual pre-training with up to 100$\\times$ fewer tokens. Our work presents a viable path toward scaling RL to pre-training levels, enabling more capable and efficient language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-13T02:08:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Search-R3: Unifying Reasoning and Embedding in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T10:25:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:44:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their remarkable natural language understanding capabilities, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been underutilized for retrieval tasks. We present Search-R3, a novel framework that addresses this limitation by adapting LLMs to generate search embeddings as a direct output of their reasoning process. Our approach exploits LLMs' chain-of-thought capabilities, allowing them to produce more effective embeddings by reasoning step-by-step through complex semantic analyses. We implement this through three complementary mechanisms. (1) a supervised learning stage enables the model's ability to produce quality embeddings, (2) a reinforcement learning (RL) methodology that optimizes embedding generation alongside reasoning, and (3) a specialized RL environment that efficiently handles evolving embedding representations without requiring complete corpus re-encoding at each training iteration. Our extensive evaluations on diverse benchmarks demonstrate that Search-R3 significantly outperforms prior methods by unifying the reasoning and embedding generation processes. This integrated post-training approach represents a substantial advancement in handling complex knowledge-intensive tasks that require both sophisticated reasoning and effective information retrieval. Project page: https://github.com/ytgui/Search-R3"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CHANGELOG: (1) Completed training of Search-R3-Large; (2) Corrected error formulation; (3) Added a `Discussion` section to the appendix. We appreciation to the anonymous reviewers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangjian","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shining","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Shining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbiao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Wenbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuli","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouxing","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhouxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PIKA: Expert-Level Synthetic Datasets for Post-Training Alignment from Scratch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:47:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T03:56:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:25:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-quality instruction data is critical for LLM alignment, yet existing open-source datasets often lack efficiency, requiring hundreds of thousands of examples to approach proprietary performance. In this work, we find that beyond the widely recognized importance of prompt-response quality, prompt difficulty itself plays a critical role in driving alignment gains. Motivated by this observation, we introduce PiKa, a data-efficient family of expert-level alignment datasets that concentrates supervision on high-difficulty instructions. The PiKa-SFT dataset contains only 30k examples, an order of magnitude fewer than state-of-the-art open datasets like Magpie-Pro. Despite its small size, fine-tuning Llama-3-8B-Base on PiKa-SFT even outperforms the official Llama-3-8B-Instruct model trained on over 10M proprietary examples on widely used benchmarks such as AlpacaEval 2.0 and Arena-Hard. We also validate the generalizability of PiKa across the Qwen2.5 series (0.5B-7B), consistently surpassing their official instruction-tuned counterparts. Additionally, we provide 30k high-quality preference optimization examples to further enhance alignment. Our results demonstrate that promising alignment is achievable with significantly reduced data, democratizing access for resource-constrained research. Our code and data will be available at https://github.com/SJY8460/PiKa."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chios","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hehai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heqing","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Heqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FURINA: A Fully Customizable Role-Playing Benchmark via Scalable Multi-Agent Collaboration Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T15:48:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:07:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T11:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) advance in role-playing (RP) tasks, existing benchmarks quickly become obsolete due to their narrow scope, outdated interaction paradigms, and limited adaptability across diverse application scenarios. To address this gap, we introduce FURINA-Builder, a novel multi-agent collaboration pipeline that automatically constructs fully customizable RP benchmarks at any scale. It enables evaluation of arbitrary characters across diverse scenarios and prompt formats, as the first benchmark builder in RP area for adaptable assessment. FURINA-Builder simulates dialogues between a test character and other characters drawn from a well-constructed character-scene pool, while an LLM judge selects fine-grained evaluation dimensions and adjusts the test character's responses into final test utterances. Using this pipeline, we build FURINA-Bench, a new comprehensive role-playing benchmark featuring both established and synthesized test characters, each assessed with dimension-specific evaluation criteria. Human evaluation and preliminary separability analysis justify our pipeline and benchmark design. We conduct extensive evaluations of cutting-edge LLMs and find that o3 and DeepSeek-R1 achieve the best performance on English and Chinese RP tasks, respectively. Across all models, established characters consistently outperform synthesized ones, with reasoning capabilities further amplifying this disparity. Interestingly, we observe that model scale does not monotonically reduce hallucinations. More critically, for reasoning LLMs, we uncover a novel trade-off: reasoning improves RP performance but simultaneously increases RP hallucinations. This trade-off extends to a broader Pareto frontier between RP performance and reliability for all LLMs. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of FURINA-Builder and the challenge posed by FURINA-Bench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:26:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07178","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07178","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07178","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imry","familyName":"Ziv","name":"Ziv, Imry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nur","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Nur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Chemla","name":"Chemla, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Biasless Language Models Learn Unnaturally: How LLMs Fail to Distinguish the Possible from the Impossible"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:17:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T16:22:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T01:08:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.eacl-long.249","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Are large language models (LLMs) sensitive to the distinction between humanly possible and impossible languages? This question was recently used in a broader debate on whether LLMs and humans share the same innate learning biases. Previous work has answered it in the positive by comparing LLM learning curves on existing language datasets and on \"impossible\" datasets derived from them via various perturbation functions. Using the same methodology, we examine this claim on a wider set of languages and impossible perturbations. We find that in most cases, GPT-2 learns each language and its impossible counterpart equally easily, in contrast to previous findings. We also apply a more lenient condition by testing whether GPT-2 provides any kind of separation between the whole sets of natural vs. impossible languages, based on cross-linguistic variance in metrics derived from the learning curves. Taken together, these perspectives show that GPT-2 provides no systematic separation between the possible and the impossible."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07178","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:43Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Penghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qianxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Effect of Attention Head Count on Transformer Approximation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:27:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T07:14:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:33:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer has become the dominant architecture for sequence modeling, yet a detailed understanding of how its structural parameters influence expressive power remains limited. In this work, we study the approximation properties of transformers, with particular emphasis on the role of the number of attention heads. Our analysis begins with the introduction of a generalized $D$-retrieval task, which we prove to be dense in the space of continuous functions, thereby providing the basis for our theoretical framework. We then establish both upper and lower bounds on the parameter complexity required for $ε$-approximation. Specifically, we show that transformers with sufficiently many heads admit efficient approximation, whereas with too few heads, the number of parameters must scale at least as $O(1/ε^{cT})$, for some constant $c$ and sequence length $T$. To the best of our knowledge, this constitutes the first rigorous lower bound of this type in a nonlinear and practically relevant setting. We further examine the single-head case and demonstrate that an embedding dimension of order $O(T)$ allows complete memorization of the input, where approximation is entirely achieved by the feed-forward block. Finally, we validate our theoretical findings with experiments on both synthetic data and real-world tasks, illustrating the practical relevance of our results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaibhav","familyName":"Srivastav","name":"Srivastav, Vaibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Bezzam","name":"Bezzam, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eustache Le","familyName":"Bihan","name":"Bihan, Eustache Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nithin Rao","familyName":"Koluguri","name":"Koluguri, Nithin Rao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Żelasko","name":"Żelasko, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somshubra","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Somshubra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel","familyName":"Moumen","name":"Moumen, Adel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanchit","familyName":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Sanchit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open ASR Leaderboard: Towards Reproducible and Transparent Multilingual and Long-Form Speech Recognition Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:44:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T07:39:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:35:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T17:30:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T01:53:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-30T09:52:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:34:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present the Open ASR Leaderboard, a reproducible benchmarking platform with community contributions from academia and industry. It compares 86 open-source and proprietary systems across 12 datasets, with English short- and long-form and multilingual short-form tracks. We standardize word error rate (WER) and inverse real-time factor (RTFx) evaluation for consistent accuracy-efficiency comparisons across model architectures and toolkits (e.g., ESPNet, NeMo, SpeechBrain, Transformers). We observe that Conformer-based encoders paired with transformer-based decoders achieve the best average WER, while connectionist temporal classification (CTC) and token-and-duration transducer (TDT) decoders offer superior RTFx, making them better suited for long-form and batched processing. All code and dataset loaders are open-sourced to support transparent, extensible evaluation. We present our evaluation methodology to facilitate community-driven benchmarking in ASR and other tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Leaderboard: https://huggingface.co/spaces/hf-audio/open_asr_leaderboard ; Code: https://github.com/huggingface/open_asr_leaderboard"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tavish","familyName":"McDonald","name":"McDonald, Tavish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav","familyName":"Fort","name":"Fort, Stanislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavya","familyName":"Kailkhura","name":"Kailkhura, Bhavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Bartoldson","name":"Bartoldson, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Get RICH or Die Scaling: Profitably Trading Inference Compute for Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:18:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T18:15:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T04:07:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time reasoning has raised benchmark performances and even shown promise in addressing the historically intractable problem of making models robust to adversarially out-of-distribution (OOD) data. Indeed, recent work used reasoning to aid satisfaction of model specifications designed to thwart attacks, finding a striking correlation between LLM reasoning effort and robustness to jailbreaks. However, this benefit fades when stronger (e.g. gradient-based or multimodal) attacks are used. This may be expected as models often can't follow instructions on the adversarially OOD data created by such attacks, and instruction following is needed to act in accordance with the attacker-thwarting spec. Thus, we hypothesize that the test-time robustness benefits of specs are unlocked by initial robustness sufficient to follow instructions on OOD data. Namely, we posit the Robustness from Inference Compute Hypothesis (RICH): inference-compute defenses profit as the model's training data better reflects the components of attacked data. Guided by the RICH, we test models of varying initial-robustness levels, finding inference-compute adds robustness even to white-box multimodal attacks, provided the model has sufficient initial robustness. Further evidencing a rich-get-richer dynamic, InternVL 3.5 gpt-oss 20B gains little robustness when its test compute is scaled, but such scaling adds significant robustness if we first robustify its vision encoder (creating the first adversarially robust reasoning VLM in the process). Robustifying models makes attacked components of data more in-distribution (ID), and the RICH suggests this fuels compositional generalization -- understanding OOD data via its ID components -- to following spec instructions on adversarial data. Consistently, we find test-time defenses both build and depend on train-time data and defenses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuyang","familyName":"Mang","name":"Mang, Qiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Runyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suyang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Suyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvin","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Alvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:29:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T22:49:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Since 2020, automated testing for Database Management Systems (DBMSs) has flourished, uncovering hundreds of bugs in widely-used systems. A cornerstone of these techniques is test oracle, which typically implements a mechanism to generate equivalent query pairs, thereby identifying bugs by checking the consistency between their results. However, while applying these oracles can be automated, their design remains a fundamentally manual endeavor. This paper explores the use of large language models (LLMs) to automate the discovery and instantiation of test oracles, addressing a long-standing bottleneck towards fully automated DBMS testing. Although LLMs demonstrate impressive creativity, they are prone to hallucinations that can produce numerous false positive bug reports. Furthermore, their significant monetary cost and latency mean that LLM invocations should be limited to ensure that bug detection is efficient and economical.\n To this end, we introduce Argus, a novel framework built upon the core concept of the Constrained Abstract Query - a SQL skeleton containing placeholders and their associated instantiation conditions (e.g., requiring a placeholder to be filled by a boolean column). Argus uses LLMs to generate pairs of these skeletons that are asserted to be semantically equivalent. This equivalence is then formally proven using a SQL equivalence solver to ensure soundness. Finally, the placeholders within the verified skeletons are instantiated with concrete, reusable SQL snippets that are also synthesized by LLMs to efficiently produce complex test cases. We implemented Argus and evaluated it on five extensively tested DBMSs, discovering 40 previously unknown bugs, 35 of which are logic bugs, with 36 confirmed and 26 already fixed by the developers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:32Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:30:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wenkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingtong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weicheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaR-KVQA: Structured Reasoning Traces for Implicit-Knowledge Visual Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T09:26:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-22T14:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge-based Visual Question Answering (KVQA) requires models to ground entities in images and reason over factual knowledge. Recent work has introduced its implicit-knowledge variant, IK-KVQA, where a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is the sole knowledge source and answers are produced without external retrieval. Existing IK-KVQA approaches, however, are typically trained with answer-only supervision: reasoning remains implicit, justifications are often weak or inconsistent, and generalization after standard supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can be brittle. We propose StaR-KVQA, a framework that equips IK-KVQA with dual-path structured reasoning traces - symbolic relation paths over text and vision together with path-grounded natural-language explanations - to provide a stronger inductive bias than generic answer-only supervision. These traces act as modality-aware scaffolds that guide the model toward relevant entities and attributes, offering more structure than generic chain-of-thought supervision while not constraining reasoning to any single fixed path. With a single open-source MLLM, StaR-KVQA constructs and selects traces to build an offline trace-enriched dataset and then performs structure-aware self-distillation; no external retrievers, verifiers, or curated knowledge bases are used, and inference is a single autoregressive pass. Across benchmarks, StaR-KVQA consistently improves both answer accuracy and the transparency of intermediate reasoning, achieving up to +11.3% higher answer accuracy on OK-VQA over the strongest baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8+3+3 pages, code: https://github.com/jianyingzhihe/StaR-KVQA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarek","familyName":"Naous","name":"Naous, Tarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Laban","name":"Laban, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Neville","name":"Neville, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flipping the Dialogue: Training and Evaluating User Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-22T13:18:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:06:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conversations with LMs involve two participants: a human user leading the conversation, and an LM assistant responding to the user's request. To satisfy this specific role, LMs are post-trained to be helpful assistants -- optimized to produce exhaustive and well-structured responses, free of ambiguity and grammar errors. User utterances, on the other hand, are rarely perfected, with each user phrasing requests in unique ways, sometimes putting in partial effort at each turn and refining on the fly. To evaluate LM performance in realistic settings, prior work simulated users in multi-turn conversations, often by prompting an LM originally trained to be a helpful assistant to act as a user. However, we show that assistant LMs make for poor user simulators, with the surprising finding that better assistants yield worse simulators. Instead, we introduce purpose-built User Language Models (User LMs) - models post-trained to simulate human users in multi-turn conversations. Through various evaluations, we show how User LMs align better with human behavior and achieve better simulation robustness than existing simulation methods. When leveraging User LMs to simulate coding and math conversations, the performance of a strong assistant (GPT-4o) drops from 74.6% to 57.4%, confirming that more realistic simulation environments lead to assistant struggles as they fail to cope with the nuances of users in multi-turn setups."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aisha","familyName":"Alansari","name":"Alansari, Aisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamzah","familyName":"Luqman","name":"Luqman, Hamzah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Hallucination: A Comprehensive Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:26:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T10:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:45:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T21:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:16:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have transformed natural language processing, achieving remarkable performance across diverse tasks. However, their impressive fluency often comes at the cost of producing false or fabricated information, a phenomenon known as hallucination. Hallucination refers to the generation of content by an LLM that is fluent and syntactically correct but factually inaccurate or unsupported by external evidence. Hallucinations undermine the reliability and trustworthiness of LLMs, especially in domains requiring factual accuracy. This survey provides a comprehensive review of research on hallucination in LLMs, with a focus on causes, detection, and mitigation. We first present a taxonomy of hallucination types and analyze their root causes across the entire LLM development lifecycle, from data collection and architecture design to inference. We further examine how hallucinations emerge in key natural language generation tasks. Building on this foundation, we introduce a structured taxonomy of detection approaches and another taxonomy of mitigation strategies. We also analyze the strengths and limitations of current detection and mitigation approaches and review existing evaluation benchmarks and metrics used to quantify LLMs hallucinations. Finally, we outline key open challenges and promising directions for future research, providing a foundation for the development of more truthful and trustworthy LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:15Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:38:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Maurer","name":"Maurer, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Snehal","familyName":"Jauhri","name":"Jauhri, Snehal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophie","familyName":"Lueth","name":"Lueth, Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgia","familyName":"Chalvatzaki","name":"Chalvatzaki, Georgia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniFField: A Generalizable Unified Neural Feature Field for Visual, Semantic, and Spatial Uncertainties in Any Scene"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:30:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:36:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T22:36:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-13T00:12:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Comprehensive visual, geometric, and semantic understanding of a 3D scene is crucial for successful execution of robotic tasks, especially in unstructured and complex environments. Additionally, to make robust decisions, it is necessary for the robot to evaluate the reliability of perceived information. While recent advances in 3D neural feature fields have enabled robots to leverage features from pretrained foundation models for tasks such as language-guided manipulation and navigation, existing methods suffer from two critical limitations: (i) they are typically scene-specific, and (ii) they lack the ability to model uncertainty in their predictions. We present UniFField, a unified uncertainty-aware neural feature field that combines visual, semantic, and geometric features in a single generalizable representation while also predicting uncertainty in each modality. Our approach, which can be applied zero shot to any new environment, incrementally integrates RGB-D images into our voxel-based feature representation as the robot explores the scene, simultaneously updating uncertainty estimation. We evaluate our uncertainty estimations to accurately describe the model prediction errors in scene reconstruction and semantic feature prediction. Furthermore, we successfully leverage our feature predictions and their respective uncertainty for an active object search task using a mobile manipulator robot, demonstrating the capability for robust decision-making."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICRA 2026 Project website: https://sites.google.com/view/uniffield"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-13T02:20:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanke","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhanke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Chentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zongze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangchao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiangchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghangfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianghangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tangyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Tangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linrui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Linrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brando","familyName":"Miranda","name":"Miranda, Brando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanmi","familyName":"Koyejo","name":"Koyejo, Sanmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AlphaApollo: A System for Deep Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:42:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:25:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present AlphaApollo, an agentic reasoning system that targets two bottlenecks in foundation-model reasoning: (1) limited reasoning capacity for complex, long-horizon problem solving and (2) unreliable test-time evolution without trustworthy verification. AlphaApollo orchestrates models and tools via three components: (i) multi-turn agentic reasoning, which formalizes model-environment interaction with structured tool calls and responses; (ii) multi-turn agentic learning, which applies turn-level reinforcement learning to optimize tool-use reasoning while decoupling actions from tool responses for stable training; and (iii) multi-round agentic evolution, which refines solutions through a propose-judge-update loop with tool-assisted verifications and long-horizon memory. Across seven math reasoning benchmarks and multiple model scales, AlphaApollo improves performance through reliable tool use (\u0026gt; 85% tool-call success), substantial gains from multi-turn RL (Avg@32: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct 1.07% -\u0026gt; 9.64%, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct 8.77% -\u0026gt; 20.35%), and improvements from evolution (e.g., Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct 5.27% -\u0026gt; 7.70%, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct 16.53% -\u0026gt; 21.08%). This project is still ongoing. We welcome feedback from the community and will frequently update the source code and technical report."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Ongoing project"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Enshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanyu","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Shanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TIGeR: Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning in Vision-Language Models for Robotics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:56:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T07:07:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in spatial reasoning, yet they remain fundamentally limited to qualitative precision and lack the computational precision required for real-world robotics. Current approaches fail to leverage metric cues from depth sensors and camera calibration, instead reducing geometric problems to pattern recognition tasks that cannot deliver the centimeter-level accuracy essential for robotic manipulation. We present TIGeR (Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning), a novel framework that transforms VLMs from perceptual estimators to geometric computers by enabling them to generate and execute precise geometric computations through external tools. Rather than attempting to internalize complex geometric operations within neural networks, TIGeR empowers models to recognize geometric reasoning requirements, synthesize appropriate computational code, and invoke specialized libraries for exact calculations. To support this paradigm, we introduce TIGeR-300K, a comprehensive tool-invocation-oriented dataset covering point transformations, pose estimation, and spatial compatibility verification, complete with tool invocation sequences and intermediate computations. Through a two-stage training pipeline combining supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) with our proposed hierarchical reward design, TIGeR achieves SOTA performance on geometric reasoning benchmarks while demonstrating centimeter-level precision in real-world robotic manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor","familyName":"Cherepanov","name":"Cherepanov, Egor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey K.","familyName":"Kovalev","name":"Kovalev, Alexey K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandr I.","familyName":"Panov","name":"Panov, Aleksandr I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ELMUR: External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite for Long-Horizon RL Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T17:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:14:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Real-world robotic agents must act under partial observability and long horizons, where key cues may appear long before they affect decision making. However, most modern approaches rely solely on instantaneous information, without incorporating insights from the past. Standard recurrent or transformer models struggle with retaining and leveraging long-term dependencies: context windows truncate history, while naive memory extensions fail under scale and sparsity. We propose ELMUR (External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite), a transformer architecture with structured external memory. Each layer maintains memory embeddings, interacts with them via bidirectional cross-attention, and updates them through an Least Recently Used (LRU) memory module using replacement or convex blending. ELMUR extends effective horizons up to 100,000 times beyond the attention window and achieves a 100% success rate on a synthetic T-Maze task with corridors up to one million steps. In POPGym, it outperforms baselines on more than half of the tasks. On MIKASA-Robo sparse-reward manipulation tasks with visual observations, it nearly doubles the performance of strong baselines, achieving the best success rate on 21 out of 23 tasks and improving the aggregate success rate across all tasks by about 70% over the previous best baseline. These results demonstrate that structured, layer-local external memory offers a simple and scalable approach to decision making under partial observability. Code and project page: https://elmur-paper.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 15 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aochong Oliver","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aochong Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanya","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Tanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Off-Trajectory Reasoning: Can LLMs Collaborate on Reasoning Trajectory?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:42:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning LLMs are trained to verbalize their reasoning process, yielding strong gains on complex tasks. This transparency also opens a promising direction: multiple reasoners can directly collaborate on each other's thinking within a shared trajectory, yielding better inference efficiency and exploration. A key prerequisite, however, is the ability to assess the usefulness and build on another model's partial thinking -- we call this off-trajectory reasoning. Our paper investigates a critical question: can standard solo-reasoning training pipelines deliver desired off-trajectory behaviors? We propose twin tests that capture the two extremes of the off-trajectory spectrum, namely Recoverability, which tests whether LLMs can backtrack from \"distractions\" induced by misleading reasoning traces, and Guidability, which tests their ability to build upon correct reasoning from stronger collaborators. Our study evaluates 15 open-weight LLMs (1.5B-32B) and reveals a counterintuitive finding -- \"stronger\" LLMs on benchmarks are often more fragile under distraction. Moreover, all models tested fail to effectively leverage guiding steps from collaborators on problems beyond their inherent capabilities with solve rates remaining under 9.2%. Finally, we conduct control studies to isolate the effects of three factors in post-training on these behaviors: the choice of distillation teacher, the use of RL, and data selection strategy. Our results provide actionable insights for training natively strong reasoning collaborators; e.g., we find that suboptimal recoverability behaviors of teacher models are transferred to distilled students even if the distillation trajectories are correct. Taken together, this work lays the groundwork for evaluating multi-model collaborations in shared reasoning trajectories and highlights the limitations of off-the-shelf reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhivar","familyName":"Sourati","name":"Sourati, Zhivar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne Menglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Marianne Menglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yazhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Mengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeeth","familyName":"Bharadwaj","name":"Bharadwaj, Sujeeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morteza","familyName":"Dehghani","name":"Dehghani, Morteza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAD-RAG: Layout-aware Dynamic RAG for Visually-Rich Document Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:02:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T00:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question answering over visually rich documents (VRDs) requires reasoning not only over isolated content but also over documents' structural organization and cross-page dependencies. However, conventional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods encode content in isolated chunks during ingestion, losing structural and cross-page dependencies, and retrieve a fixed number of pages at inference, regardless of the specific demands of the question or context. This often results in incomplete evidence retrieval and degraded answer quality for multi-page reasoning tasks. To address these limitations, we propose LAD-RAG, a novel Layout-Aware Dynamic RAG framework. During ingestion, LAD-RAG constructs a symbolic document graph that captures layout structure and cross-page dependencies, adding it alongside standard neural embeddings to yield a more holistic representation of the document. During inference, an LLM agent dynamically interacts with the neural and symbolic indices to adaptively retrieve the necessary evidence based on the query. Experiments on MMLongBench-Doc, LongDocURL, DUDE, and MP-DocVQA demonstrate that LAD-RAG improves retrieval, achieving over 90% perfect recall on average without any top-k tuning, and outperforming baseline retrievers by up to 20% in recall at comparable noise levels, yielding higher QA accuracy with minimal latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06823","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riku","familyName":"Mochizuki","name":"Mochizuki, Riku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusuke","familyName":"Komatsu","name":"Komatsu, Shusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souta","familyName":"Noguchi","name":"Noguchi, Souta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuto","familyName":"Ataka","name":"Ataka, Kazuto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:47:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T03:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze answers generated by generative engines (GEs) from the perspectives of citation publishers and the content-injection barrier, defined as the difficulty for attackers to manipulate answers to user prompts by placing malicious content on the web. GEs integrate two functions: web search and answer generation that cites web pages using large language models. Because anyone can publish information on the web, GEs are vulnerable to poisoning attacks. Existing studies of citation evaluation focus on how faithfully answer content reflects cited sources, leaving unexamined which web sources should be selected as citations to defend against poisoning attacks. To fill this gap, we introduce evaluation criteria that assess poisoning threats using the citation information contained in answers. Our criteria classify the publisher attributes of citations to estimate the content-injection barrier thereby revealing the threat of poisoning attacks in current GEs. We conduct experiments in political domains in Japan and the United States (U.S.) using our criteria and show that citations from official party websites (primary sources) are approximately \\(25\\%\\)--\\(45\\%\\) in the U.S. and \\(60\\%\\)--\\(65\\%\\) in Japan, indicating that U.S. political answers are at higher risk of poisoning attacks. We also find that sources with low content-injection barriers are frequently cited yet are poorly reflected in answer content. To mitigate this threat, we discuss how publishers of primary sources can increase exposure of their web content in answers and show that well-known techniques are limited by language differences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, under-reviewing at a conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06823","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niharika","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Niharika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamara","familyName":"Zubatiy","name":"Zubatiy, Tamara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agata","familyName":"Rozga","name":"Rozga, Agata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jodi","familyName":"Forlizzi","name":"Forlizzi, Jodi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizabeth","familyName":"Mynatt","name":"Mynatt, Elizabeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sometimes You Need Facts, and Sometimes a Hug: Understanding Older Adults' Preferences for Explanations in LLM-Based Conversational AI Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:44:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T07:20:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3790812","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing Conversational AI systems to support older adults requires these systems to explain their behavior in ways that align with older adults' preferences and context. While prior work has emphasized the importance of AI explainability in building user trust, relatively little is known about older adults' requirements and perceptions of AI-generated explanations. To address this gap, we conducted an exploratory Speed Dating study with 23 older adults to understand their responses to contextually grounded AI explanations. Our findings reveal the highly context-dependent nature of explanations, shaped by conversational cues such as the content, tone, and framing of explanation. We also found that explanations are often interpreted as interactive, multi-turn conversational exchanges with the AI, and can be helpful in calibrating urgency, guiding actionability, and providing insights into older adults' daily lives for their family members. We conclude by discussing implications for designing context-sensitive and personalized explanations in Conversational AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be published at ACM CHI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valter","familyName":"Hudovernik","name":"Hudovernik, Valter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Znidar","name":"Znidar, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charilaos","familyName":"Kanatsoulis","name":"Kanatsoulis, Charilaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshan","familyName":"Upendra","name":"Upendra, Roshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Palczewski","name":"Palczewski, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Guestrin","name":"Guestrin, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jure","familyName":"Leskovec","name":"Leskovec, Jure","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relational Transformer: Toward Zero-Shot Foundation Models for Relational Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:51:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T22:13:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:10:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T07:22:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:45:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained transformers readily adapt to new sequence modeling tasks via zero-shot prompting, but relational domains still lack architectures that transfer across datasets and tasks. The core challenge is the diversity of relational data, with varying heterogeneous schemas, graph structures and functional dependencies. In this paper, we present the Relational Transformer (RT) architecture, which can be pretrained on diverse relational databases and directly applied to unseen datasets and tasks without task- or dataset-specific fine-tuning, or retrieval of in-context examples. RT (i) incorporates task specification via task table prompting, (ii) tokenizes cells with table/column metadata, (iii) is pretrained via masked token prediction, and (iv) utilizes a novel Relational Attention mechanism over columns, rows, and primary-foreign key links. Pretrained on RelBench datasets spanning tasks such as churn and sales forecasting, RT attains strong zero-shot performance, averaging 93% of fully supervised AUROC on binary classification tasks with a single forward pass of a 22M parameter model, as opposed to 84% for a 27B LLM. Fine-tuning yields state-of-the-art results with high sample efficiency. Our experimental analyses show that RT's zero-shot transfer leverages task context, relational attention patterns and schema semantics. Overall, RT provides a practical path toward foundation models for relational data. Code, models, data: https://github.com/snap-stanford/relational-transformer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChainMPQ: Interleaved Text-Image Reasoning Chains for Mitigating Relation Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:19:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T01:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal tasks, hallucinations continue to hinder their reliability. Among the three categories of hallucinations, which include object, attribute, and relation, relation hallucinations account for the largest proportion but have received the least attention. To address this issue, we propose ChainMPQ (Multi-Perspective Questions guided Interleaved Chain of Image and Text), a training-free method that improves relational inference in LVLMs by utilizing accumulated textual and visual memories. ChainMPQ first extracts subject and object keywords from the question to enhance the corresponding image regions. It then constructs multi-perspective questions that focus on the three core components of a relationship: the subject, the object, and the relation that links them. These questions are sequentially input to the model, with textual and visual memories from earlier steps providing supporting context for subsequent ones, thereby forming an interleaved chain of images and text that guides progressive relational reasoning. Experiments on multiple LVLMs and benchmarks show that ChainMPQ substantially reduces relation hallucinations, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of its three core modules."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06730","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06730","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06730","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Frank","name":"Frank, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haithem","familyName":"Afli","name":"Afli, Haithem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PTEB: Towards Robust Text Embedding Evaluation via Stochastic Paraphrasing at Evaluation Time with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:37:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:30:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T18:03:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:57:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T16:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:55:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current sentence embedding evaluations typically rely on static test beds like the Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB). While invaluable, repeated tuning on a fixed suite can inflate reported scores and obscure real-world robustness. We introduce the Paraphrasing Text Embedding Benchmark (PTEB), a dynamic protocol that stochastically generates meaning-preserving paraphrases at evaluation time and aggregates results across multiple runs. Using a cost-efficient LLM-based method grounded in gold ratings and human validation, we show that LLMs generate token-diverse but semantically preserving paraphrases. Across 7 MTEB tasks, we validate our hypothesis that the performance of sentence encoders is sensitive to changes in token space even when semantics remain fixed. We also observe that smaller models are not disproportionately affected relative to larger ones. Our results are statistically robust over multiple runs spanning 20 datasets and 25 languages. More generally, we aim to propose a new evaluation paradigm in NLP that relies less on static, pre-defined benchmarks but shifts towards dynamic, stochastic evaluation leveraging eval-time compute. We make the code to run PTEB publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06730","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angie","familyName":"Boggust","name":"Boggust, Angie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghao","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Donghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannick","familyName":"Assogba","name":"Assogba, Yannick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Moritz","name":"Moritz, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arvind","familyName":"Satyanarayan","name":"Satyanarayan, Arvind","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fred","familyName":"Hohman","name":"Hohman, Fred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic Regexes: Auto-Interpreting LLM Features with a Structured Language"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:56:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T22:01:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:07:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated interpretability aims to translate large language model (LLM) features into human understandable descriptions. However, natural language feature descriptions can be vague, inconsistent, and require manual relabeling. In response, we introduce semantic regexes, structured language descriptions of LLM features. By combining primitives that capture linguistic and semantic patterns with modifiers for contextualization, composition, and quantification, semantic regexes produce precise and expressive feature descriptions. Across quantitative benchmarks and qualitative analyses, semantic regexes match the accuracy of natural language while yielding more concise and consistent feature descriptions. Their inherent structure affords new types of analyses, including quantifying feature complexity across layers, scaling automated interpretability from insights into individual features to model-wide patterns. Finally, in user studies, we find that semantic regexes help people build accurate mental models of LLM features."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelvin Kiu-Wai","familyName":"Tam","name":"Tam, Kelvin Kiu-Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newt Hue-Nam K.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Newt Hue-Nam K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Tianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ginny Y.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ginny Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"See","name":"See, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NewtonBench: Benchmarking Generalizable Scientific Law Discovery in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:12:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T09:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are emerging as powerful tools for scientific law discovery, a foundational challenge in AI-driven science. However, existing benchmarks for this task suffer from a fundamental methodological trilemma, forcing a trade-off between scientific relevance, scalability, and resistance to memorization. Furthermore, they oversimplify discovery as static function fitting, failing to capture the authentic scientific process of uncovering embedded laws through the interactive exploration of complex model systems. To address these critical gaps, we introduce NewtonBench, a benchmark comprising 324 scientific law discovery tasks across 12 physics domains. Our design mitigates the evaluation trilemma by using counterfactual law shifts - systematic alterations of canonical laws - to generate a vast suite of problems that are scalable, scientifically relevant, and memorization-resistant. Moreover, we elevate the evaluation from static function fitting to interactive model discovery, requiring agents to experimentally probe simulated complex systems to uncover hidden principles. Our extensive experiment reveals a clear but fragile capability for discovery in frontier LLMs: this ability degrades precipitously with increasing system complexity and exhibits extreme sensitivity to observational noise. Notably, we uncover a paradoxical effect of tool assistance: providing a code interpreter can hinder more capable models by inducing a premature shift from exploration to exploitation, causing them to satisfice on suboptimal solutions. These results demonstrate that robust, generalizable discovery in complex, interactive environments remains the core challenge. By providing a scalable, robust, and scientifically authentic testbed, NewtonBench offers a crucial tool for measuring true progress and guiding the development of next-generation AI agents capable of genuine scientific discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Boyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouhan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhouhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AWM: Accurate Weight-Matrix Fingerprint for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:51:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:47:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-14T13:03:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protecting the intellectual property of large language models (LLMs) is crucial, given the substantial resources required for their training. Consequently, there is an urgent need for both model owners and third parties to determine whether a suspect LLM is trained from scratch or derived from an existing base model. However, the intensive post-training processes that models typically undergo-such as supervised fine-tuning, extensive continued pretraining, reinforcement learning, multi-modal extension, pruning, and upcycling-pose significant challenges to reliable identification. In this work, we propose a training-free fingerprinting method based on weight matrices. We leverage the Linear Assignment Problem (LAP) and an unbiased Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) similarity to neutralize the effects of parameter manipulations, yielding a highly robust and high-fidelity similarity metric. On a comprehensive testbed of 60 positive and 90 negative model pairs, our method demonstrates exceptional robustness against all six aforementioned post-training categories while exhibiting a near-zero risk of false positives. By achieving perfect scores on all classification metrics, our approach establishes a strong basis for reliable model lineage verification. Moreover, the entire computation completes within 30s on an NVIDIA 3090 GPU. The code is available at https://github.com/LUMIA-Group/AWM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Kayaalp","name":"Kayaalp, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caner","familyName":"Turkmen","name":"Turkmen, Caner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleksandr","familyName":"Shchur","name":"Shchur, Oleksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Mercado","name":"Mercado, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Fatir","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Abdul Fatir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Bohlke-Schneider","name":"Bohlke-Schneider, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bernie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Efficient Pretrained Model Portfolios for Time Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:57:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T21:43:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:10:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Is bigger always better for time series foundation models? With the question in mind, we explore an alternative to training a single, large monolithic model: building a portfolio of smaller, pretrained forecasting models. By applying ensembling or model selection over these portfolios, we achieve competitive performance on large-scale benchmarks using much fewer parameters. We explore strategies for designing such portfolios and find that collections of specialist models consistently outperform portfolios of independently trained generalists. Remarkably, we demonstrate that post-training a base model is a compute-effective approach for creating sufficiently diverse specialists, and provide evidences that ensembling and model selection are more compute-efficient than test-time fine-tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as an ICLR 2026 conference paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique","familyName":"Queipo-de-Llano","name":"Queipo-de-Llano, Enrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro","familyName":"Arroyo","name":"Arroyo, Álvaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Barbero","name":"Barbero, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Bronstein","name":"Bronstein, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yann","familyName":"LeCun","name":"LeCun, Yann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravid","familyName":"Shwartz-Ziv","name":"Shwartz-Ziv, Ravid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Attention Sinks and Compression Valleys in LLMs are Two Sides of the Same Coin"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:27:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:10:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T21:51:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:09:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Attention sinks and compression valleys have attracted significant attention as two puzzling phenomena in large language models, but have been studied in isolation. In this work, we present a surprising connection between attention sinks and compression valleys, tracing both to the formation of massive activations in the residual stream. We prove theoretically that massive activations necessarily produce representational compression and establish bounds on the resulting entropy reduction. Through experiments across several models (410M-120B parameters), we confirm that when the beginning-of-sequence token develops extreme activation norms in the middle layers, both compression valleys and attention sinks emerge simultaneously. Targeted ablation studies validate our theoretical predictions. This unified view motivates us to propose the Mix-Compress-Refine theory of information flow, as an attempt to explain how LLMs organize their computation in depth by controlling attention and representational compression via massive activations. Specifically, we posit that Transformer-based LLMs process tokens in three distinct phases: (1) broad mixing in the early layers, (2) compressed computation with limited mixing in the middle layers, and (3) selective refinement in the late layers. Our framework helps explain why embedding tasks perform best at intermediate layers, whereas generation tasks benefit from full-depth processing, clarifying differences in task-dependent representations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:12Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:29:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06548","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06548","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06548","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seng Pei","familyName":"Liew","name":"Liew, Seng Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuya","familyName":"Kato","name":"Kato, Takuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reusing Overtrained Language Models Saturates Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:15:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T04:23:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:30:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reusing pretrained base models for further pretraining, such as continual pretraining or model growth, is promising at reducing the cost of training language models from scratch. However, the effectiveness remains unclear, especially when applied to overtrained base models. In this work, we empirically study the scaling properties of model reuse and find that the scaling efficiency diminishes in a predictable manner: The scaling exponent with respect to second-stage training tokens decreases logarithmically with the number of tokens used to pretrain the base model.\n The joint dependence on first- and second-stage tokens is accurately modeled by a simple scaling law.\n Such saturation effect reveals a fundamental trade-off in multi-stage pretraining strategies: the more extensively a base model is pretrained, the less benefit additional pretraining provides.\n Our findings provide practical insights for efficient language model training and raise important considerations for the reuse of overtrained models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06548","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:50Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:41:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhi","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Chengzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianying","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qianying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yugo","familyName":"Murawaki","name":"Murawaki, Yugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadao","familyName":"Kurohashi","name":"Kurohashi, Sadao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Lives in Sparse Dimensions: Toward Interpretable and Efficient Multilingual Control for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:46:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T07:52:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:34:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models exhibit strong multilingual capabilities despite limited exposure to non-English data. Prior studies show that English-centric large language models map multilingual content into English-aligned representations at intermediate layers and then project them back into target-language token spaces in the final layer. From this observation, we hypothesize that this cross-lingual transition is governed by a small and sparse set of dimensions, which occur at consistent indices across the intermediate to final layers. Building on this insight, we introduce a simple, training-free method to identify and manipulate these dimensions, requiring only as few as 50 sentences of either parallel or monolingual data. Experiments on a multilingual generation control task reveal the interpretability of these dimensions, demonstrating that the interventions in these dimensions can switch the output language while preserving semantic content, and that it surpasses the performance of prior neuron-based approaches at a substantially lower cost."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EACL 2026 (Main). Our code will be available at: https://github.com/ku-nlp/language-specific-dimensions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:42:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06924","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Almas","familyName":"Baimagambetov","name":"Baimagambetov, Almas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Avgerinakis","name":"Avgerinakis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Polatidis","name":"Polatidis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ethical AI prompt recommendations in large language models using collaborative filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:03:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) shape AI development, ensuring ethical prompt recommendations is crucial. LLMs offer innovation but risk bias, fairness issues, and accountability concerns. Traditional oversight methods struggle with scalability, necessitating dynamic solutions. This paper proposes using collaborative filtering, a technique from recommendation systems, to enhance ethical prompt selection. By leveraging user interactions, it promotes ethical guidelines while reducing bias. Contributions include a synthetic dataset for prompt recommendations and the application of collaborative filtering. The work also tackles challenges in ethical AI, such as bias mitigation, transparency, and preventing unethical prompt engineering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to by the International Journal of Parallel, Emergent \u0026amp; Distributed Systems (Taylor and Francis) and has an assigned DOI. We have already chose to make this open access using CC BY. The article is not yet available online on the publisher's website. The DOI is: doi.org/10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06924","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:36:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwei","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Hongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reading Between the Lines: Towards Reliable Black-box LLM Fingerprinting via Zeroth-order Gradient Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:27:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T12:56:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:43:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792196","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The substantial investment required to develop Large Language Models (LLMs) makes them valuable intellectual property, raising significant concerns about copyright protection. LLM fingerprinting has emerged as a key technique to address this, which aims to verify a model's origin by extracting an intrinsic, unique signature (a \"fingerprint\") and comparing it to that of a source model to identify illicit copies. However, existing black-box fingerprinting methods often fail to generate distinctive LLM fingerprints. This ineffectiveness arises because black-box methods typically rely on model outputs, which lose critical information about the model's unique parameters due to the usage of non-linear functions. To address this, we first leverage Fisher Information Theory to formally demonstrate that the gradient of the model's input is a more informative feature for fingerprinting than the output. Based on this insight, we propose ZeroPrint, a novel method that approximates these information-rich gradients in a black-box setting using zeroth-order estimation. ZeroPrint overcomes the challenge of applying this to discrete text by simulating input perturbations via semantic-preserving word substitutions. This operation allows ZeroPrint to estimate the model's Jacobian matrix as a unique fingerprint. Experiments on the standard benchmark show ZeroPrint achieves a state-of-the-art effectiveness and robustness, significantly outperforming existing black-box methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepeted by the ACM Web Conference (WWW) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:18:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07238","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunyi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xunyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Dingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Benchmarks Age: Temporal Misalignment through Large Language Model Factuality Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:06:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T17:39:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) and the real world has outpaced the static nature of widely used evaluation benchmarks, raising concerns about their reliability for evaluating LLM factuality. While substantial works continue to rely on the popular but old benchmarks, their temporal misalignment with real-world facts and modern LLMs, and their effects on LLM factuality evaluation remain underexplored. Therefore, in this work, we present a systematic investigation of this issue by examining five popular factuality benchmarks and eight LLMs released across different years. An up-to-date fact retrieval pipeline and three metrics are tailored to quantify benchmark aging and its impact on LLM factuality evaluation. Experimental results and analysis illustrate that a considerable portion of samples in the widely used factuality benchmarks are outdated, leading to unreliable assessments of LLM factuality. We hope our work can provide a testbed to assess the reliability of a benchmark for LLM factuality evaluation and inspire more research on the benchmark aging issue. Codes are available in https://github.com/JiangXunyi/BenchAge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EACL 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07238","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Handong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushal","familyName":"Kafle","name":"Kafle, Kushal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Kuen","name":"Kuen, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoT Referring: Improving Referring Expression Tasks with Grounded Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:50:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Expression Comprehension and Segmentation are critical tasks for assessing the integration of language understanding and image comprehension, serving as benchmarks for Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) capabilities. To address these challenges, we propose a new strategy, CoT Referring, which enhances model reasoning across modalities through a structured, chain-of-thought training data structure. Our approach systematically parses textual structures to a sequential referring step, where in each step it identifies relationships and ensures consistent reference alignment, thereby improving accuracy in complex query scenarios. We restructure the training data to enforce a new output form, providing new annotations for existing datasets and compiling an evaluation benchmark from existing resources. This benchmark is designed explicitly for complex referring cases. We also integrate detection and segmentation capabilities into a unified MLLM framework, training it with a novel adaptive weighted loss to optimize performance. Experimental results on our curated benchmark and RefCOCO/+/g demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with a notable increase of 2.5%+ over baseline models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MLLM, Referring Expression Segmentation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahe","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiahe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijay","familyName":"Paladugu","name":"Paladugu, Abhijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beneficial Reasoning Behaviors in Agentic Search and Effective Post-training to Obtain Them"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T06:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T18:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:49:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic search requires large language models (LLMs) to perform multi-step search to solve complex information-seeking tasks, imposing unique challenges on their reasoning capabilities. However, what constitutes effective reasoning for agentic search and how it can be learned remains unclear. In this work, we first investigate the reasoning behaviors that enable success in agentic search. By comparing successful and failed trajectories via an LLM-based analysis pipeline, we identify four beneficial behaviors: Information Verification, Authority Evaluation, Adaptive Search, and Error Recovery. Building on this, we propose Behavior Priming, a training approach that equips agentic search models with these reasoning behaviors before reinforcement learning (RL). Specifically, it first performs supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on collected trajectories exhibiting the identified behaviors to cultivate these behaviors, and then applies standard RL to further improve task performance. Experiments on Qwen3-1.7B and Llama3.2-3B-Instruct show that Behavior Priming yields relative improvements over direct RL by 37.2\\% on three web benchmarks and 6.2\\% on seven multi-hop QA benchmarks, and outperforms the SFT-then-RL baseline using outcome-correct trajectories for fine-tuning. Crucially, we show that these reasoning behaviors matter more than outcome correctness in the priming stage prior to RL. Further analysis reveals that Behavior Priming enhances exploration (pass@8) and test-time scaling (search step number), providing a robust foundation for RL. Our code are avalible at https://github.com/cxcscmu/Behavior-Priming-for-Agentic-Search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Hommel","name":"Hommel, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuf Denizay","familyName":"Dönder","name":"Dönder, Yusuf Denizay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungyong","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Sungyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Mimno","name":"Mimno, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Unso Eun Seo","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Unso Eun Seo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent Bain vs. Agent McKinsey: A New Text-to-SQL Benchmark for the Business Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-11T00:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:37:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T22:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-SQL benchmarks have traditionally only tested simple data access as a translation task of natural language to SQL queries. But in reality, users tend to ask diverse questions that require more complex responses including data-driven predictions or recommendations. Using the business domain as a motivating example, we introduce CORGI, a new benchmark that expands text-to-SQL to reflect practical database queries encountered by end users. CORGI is composed of synthetic databases inspired by enterprises such as DoorDash, Airbnb, and Lululemon. It provides questions across four increasingly complicated categories of business queries: descriptive, explanatory, predictive, and recommendational. This challenge calls for causal reasoning, temporal forecasting, and strategic recommendation, reflecting multi-level and multi-step agentic intelligence. We find that LLM performance degrades on higher-level questions as question complexity increases. CORGI also introduces and encourages the text-to-SQL community to consider new automatic methods for evaluating open-ended, qualitative responses in data access tasks. Our experiments show that LLMs exhibit an average 33.12% lower success execution rate (SER) on CORGI compared to existing benchmarks such as BIRD, highlighting the substantially higher complexity of real-world business needs. We release the CORGI dataset, an evaluation framework, and a submission website to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, under review for ACL ARR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07227","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07227","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07227","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Krishnakumar","name":"Krishnakumar, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rhea Sanjay","familyName":"Sukthanker","name":"Sukthanker, Rhea Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannan Javed","familyName":"Mahadik","name":"Mahadik, Hannan Javed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriela","familyName":"Kadlecová","name":"Kadlecová, Gabriela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladyslav","familyName":"Moroshan","name":"Moroshan, Vladyslav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Carstensen","name":"Carstensen, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Hutter","name":"Hutter, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where to Begin: Efficient Pretraining via Subnetwork Selection and Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:57:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T11:05:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small Language models (SLMs) offer an efficient and accessible alternative to Large Language Models (LLMs), delivering strong performance while using far fewer resources. We introduce a simple and effective framework for pretraining SLMs that brings together three complementary ideas. First, we identify structurally sparse sub-network initializations that consistently outperform randomly initialized models of similar size under the same compute budget. Second, we use evolutionary search to automatically discover high-quality sub-network initializations, providing better starting points for pretraining. Third, we apply knowledge distillation from larger teacher models to speed up training and improve generalization. Together, these components make SLM pretraining substantially more efficient: our best model, discovered using evolutionary search and initialized with LLM weights, matches the validation perplexity of a comparable Pythia SLM while requiring 5.16x and 1.26x fewer floating point operations for token budgets of 10B and 100B, respectively. We release all code publicly, offering a practical and reproducible path toward cost-efficient small language model development at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07227","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Giordano","name":"Giordano, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Razniewski","name":"Razniewski, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foundations of LLM Knowledge Materialization: Termination, Reproducibility, Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:34:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T11:00:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:47:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T10:18:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-14T01:34:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) encode substantial factual knowledge, yet measuring and systematizing this knowledge remains challenging. Converting it into structured format, for example through recursive extraction approaches such as the GPTKB methodology (Hu et al., 2025b), is still underexplored. Key open questions include whether such extraction can terminate, whether its outputs are reproducible, and how robust they are to variations. We systematically study LLM knowledge materialization using miniGPTKBs (domain-specific, tractable subcrawls), analyzing termination, reproducibility, and robustness across three categories of metrics: yield, lexical similarity, and semantic similarity. We experiment with four variations (seed, language, randomness, model) and three illustrative domains (from history, entertainment, and finance). Our findings show (i) high termination rates, though model-dependent; (ii) mixed reproducibility; and (iii) robustness that varies by perturbation type: high for seeds and temperature, lower for languages and models. These results suggest that LLM knowledge materialization can reliably surface core knowledge, while also revealing important limitations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted and published in Findings of EACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-14T03:09:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization: Synergizing Reasoning and Adaptive Tool Use with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T08:14:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:09:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have popularized test-time scaling, where models generate additional reasoning tokens before producing final answers. These approaches have demonstrated significant performance improvements on benchmarks involving mathematical reasoning. However, language models relying solely on direct inference still struggle with tasks demanding up-to-date knowledge or computational tools such as calculators and code interpreters for complex arithmetic operations. To overcome these limitations, we propose Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization (TAPO), a novel reinforcement learning framework that systematically integrates multi-hop reasoning with adaptive tool-calling capabilities. Our approach employs a modified version of Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization (DAPO), a recently developed RL paradigm, which we adapt specifically for tool invocation scenarios, enabling models to dynamically interleave complex reasoning with on-demand tool usage (including search APIs and Python interpreters).\n To support this research, we introduce two new datasets: TAPO-easy-60K and TAPO-hard-18K, specifically designed to train and evaluate both fact-based reasoning and mathematical calculation capabilities. Our experiments on Qwen2.5-3B and Qwen2.5-7B models demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with both models achieving state-of-the-art performance on tasks requiring external knowledge and mathematical computation among methods with comparable parameters. Notably, TAPO achieves more efficient tool utilization than baseline methods while preventing excessive calls caused by reward hacking. These results highlight the significant potential of combining advanced reasoning with tool usage to enhance model performance in knowledge-intensive and computationally demanding tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06747","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06747","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06747","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I-Fan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, I-Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faegheh","familyName":"Hasibi","name":"Hasibi, Faegheh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suzan","familyName":"Verberne","name":"Verberne, Suzan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs Enable Bag-of-Texts Representations for Short-Text Clustering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:05:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:45:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T14:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:26:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose a training-free method for unsupervised short text clustering that relies less on careful selection of embedders than other methods. In customer-facing chatbots, companies are dealing with large amounts of user utterances that need to be clustered according to their intent. In these settings, no labeled data is typically available, and the number of clusters is not known. Recent approaches to short-text clustering in label-free settings incorporate LLM output to refine existing embeddings. While LLMs can identify similar texts effectively, the resulting similarities may not be directly represented by distances in the dense vector space, as they depend on the original embedding. We therefore propose a method for transforming LLM judgments directly into a bag-of-texts representation in which texts are initialized to be equidistant, without assuming any prior distance relationships. Our method achieves comparable or superior results to state-of-the-art methods, but without embeddings optimization or assuming prior knowledge of clusters or labels. Experiments on diverse datasets and smaller LLMs show that our method is model agnostic and can be applied to any embedder, with relatively small LLMs, and different clustering methods. We also show how our method scales to large datasets, reducing the computational cost of the LLM use. The flexibility and scalability of our method make it more aligned with real-world training-free scenarios than existing clustering methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06747","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjeda","familyName":"Akter","name":"Akter, Sanjeda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibne Farabi","familyName":"Shihab","name":"Shihab, Ibne Farabi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anuj","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Anuj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anytime-Valid Answer Sufficiency Certificates for LLM Generation via Sequential Information Lift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:28:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-05T17:33:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-06T02:28:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Sequential-EDFL (Empirical Dynamic Formal Lift), which applies anytime-valid sequential testing to language model generation stopping. Our approach tracks information lift, defined as the log-likelihood ratio between the full model and deliberately weakened \"skeleton\" baselines, using self-normalized empirical-Bernstein e-processes that provide formal delta-level error control regardless of stopping time. This delta guarantee controls premature stopping when information lift is insufficient relative to the skeleton, and it does not imply delta control of factual incorrectness or hallucinations. We handle unknown centering through online mean estimation, combine multiple parameters via mixture e-processes, and support adaptive resets under distributional drift. On six benchmarks, Sequential-EDFL reduces generation length by 22 to 28 percent relative to sequential baselines while maintaining delta-level control with 12 percent computational overhead. We introduce automated skeletons (distilled submodels and randomized logits) and show robustness across skeleton families. Composing EDFL with a lightweight correctness gate (sentence boundaries plus a verifier) improves end-task correctness while preserving anytime-valid guarantees by only delaying stopping. Our certificates control information sufficiency, not factual correctness. Specifically, 10.9 percent of stopped sequences remain incorrect even with the gate (13.2 to 22.7 percent without it). EDFL serves as a first-stage filter that can reduce verification burden: when applied to stopped sequences, the gate validates 83 percent of stops, requiring full verification only for the remaining 17 percent, plus all non-stopped sequences. EDFL is not a standalone solution for safety-critical domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:13Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:10:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Hippocampus Networks for Efficient Long-Context Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T07:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-sequence modeling faces a fundamental trade-off between the efficiency of compressive fixed-size memory in RNN-like models and the fidelity of lossless growing memory in attention-based Transformers. Inspired by the Multi-Store Model in cognitive science, we introduce a memory framework of artificial neural networks. Our method maintains a sliding window of the Transformer's KV cache as lossless short-term memory, while a learnable module termed Artificial Hippocampus Network (AHN) recurrently compresses out-of-window information into a fixed-size compact long-term memory. To validate this framework, we instantiate AHNs using modern RNN-like architectures, including Mamba2, DeltaNet, and GatedDeltaNet to augment open-weight LLMs. We also propose an efficient self-distillation training method where the base model's all parameters are frozen and only the parameters from AHNs are optimized. For inference, our method sets a default large sliding window size of 32k for attention, and AHNs activate only when the sequence length exceeds the 32k window, addressing the quadratic-complexity issue of attention that emerges at that scale. Extensive experiments on long-context benchmarks LV-Eval and InfiniteBench demonstrate that AHN-augmented models consistently outperform sliding window baselines and achieve performance comparable or even superior to full-attention models, while substantially reducing computational and memory requirements. For instance, augmenting the Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct with AHNs reduces inference FLOPs by 40.5% and memory cache by 74.0%, while improving its average score on LV-Eval (128k sequence length) from 4.41 to 5.88. Code is available at: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun Wei","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yun Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HTMformer: Hybrid Time and Multivariate Transformer for Time Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:52:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T03:57:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:20:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T10:16:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:24:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based methods have achieved impressive results in time series forecasting. However, existing Transformers still exhibit limitations in sequence modeling as they tend to overemphasize temporal dependencies. This incurs additional computational overhead without yielding corresponding performance gains. We find that the performance of Transformers is highly dependent on the embedding method used to learn effective representations. To address this issue, we extract multivariate features to augment the effective information captured in the embedding layer, yielding multidimensional embeddings that convey richer and more meaningful sequence representations. These representations enable Transformer-based forecasters to better understand the series. Specifically, we introduce Hybrid Temporal and Multivariate Embeddings (HTME). The HTME extractor integrates a lightweight temporal feature extraction module with a carefully designed multivariate feature extraction module to provide complementary features, thereby achieving a balance between model complexity and performance. By combining HTME with the Transformer architecture, we present HTMformer, leveraging the enhanced feature extraction capability of the HTME extractor to build a lightweight forecaster. Experiments conducted on eight real-world datasets demonstrate that our approach outperforms existing baselines in both accuracy and efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06249","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshiki","familyName":"Nakai","name":"Nakai, Toshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi Kiran","familyName":"Chikkala","name":"Chikkala, Ravi Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lena Sophie","familyName":"Oberkircher","name":"Oberkircher, Lena Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Jennings","name":"Jennings, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalia","familyName":"Skachkova","name":"Skachkova, Natalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Anikina","name":"Anikina, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesujoba Oluwadara","familyName":"Alabi","name":"Alabi, Jesujoba Oluwadara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRepLiNa: Layer-wise CKA+REPINA Alignment Improves Low-Resource Machine Translation in Aya-23 8B"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T17:36:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T15:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:39:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T19:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T16:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:40:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-10T18:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-11T01:54:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The 2025 Multimodal Models for Low-Resource Contexts and Social Impact (MMLoSo) Language Challenge addresses one of India's most pressing linguistic gaps: the lack of resources for its diverse low-resource languages (LRLs). In this study, we investigate whether enforcing cross-lingual similarity in specific internal layers of a decoder-only multilingual large language model (LLM) can improve translation quality from LRL to high-resource language (HRL). Specifically, we combine Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), a similarity metric that encourages representations of different languages to align, with REPINA, a regularization method that constrains parameter updates to remain close to the pretrained model, into a joint method we call TRepLiNa. In this research project, we experiment with zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning settings using Aya-23 8B with QLoRA across MMLoSo shared task language pairs (Mundari, Santali, Bhili) with Hindi/English pivots. Our results show that aligning mid-level layers using TRepLiNa (CKA+REPINA) is a low-cost, practical approach to improving LRL translation, especially in data-scarce settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"It is work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06249","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-11T03:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06783","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06783","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06783","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshit","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Akshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shyam","familyName":"Marjit","name":"Marjit, Shyam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Gavrikov","name":"Gavrikov, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Yeung-Levy","name":"Yeung-Levy, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilde","familyName":"Kuehne","name":"Kuehne, Hilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rogerio","familyName":"Feris","name":"Feris, Rogerio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sivan","familyName":"Doveh","name":"Doveh, Sivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Glass","name":"Glass, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Jehanzeb","familyName":"Mirza","name":"Mirza, M. Jehanzeb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TTRV: Test-Time Reinforcement Learning for Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T13:17:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:41:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing methods for extracting reward signals in Reinforcement Learning typically rely on labeled data and dedicated training splits, a setup that contrasts with how humans learn directly from their environment. In this work, we propose TTRV to enhance vision language understanding by adapting the model on the fly at inference time, without the need for any labeled data. Concretely, we enhance the Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) framework by designing rewards based on the frequency of the base model's output, while inferring on each test sample multiple times. Further, we also propose to control the diversity of the model's output by simultaneously rewarding the model for obtaining low entropy of the output empirical distribution. Our approach delivers consistent gains across both object recognition and visual question answering (VQA), with improvements of up to 52.4% and 29.8%, respectively, and average boosts of 24.6% and 10.0% across 16 datasets. Remarkably, on image recognition, TTRV applied to InternVL 8B surpasses GPT-4o by an average of 2.3% over 8 benchmarks, while remaining highly competitive on VQA, demonstrating that test-time reinforcement learning can match or exceed the strongest proprietary models. Finally, we find many interesting properties of test-time RL for VLMs: for example, even in extremely data-constrained scenarios, where adaptation is performed on a single randomly chosen unlabeled test example, TTRV still yields non-trivial improvements of up to 5.5% in recognition tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06783","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-05T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06349","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moein E.","familyName":"Samadi","name":"Samadi, Moein E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Schuppert","name":"Schuppert, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible Swarm Learning May Outpace Foundation Models in Essential Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T11:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:45:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have rapidly advanced AI, raising the question of whether their decisions will ultimately surpass human strategies in real-world domains. The exponential, and possibly super-exponential, pace of AI development makes such analysis elusive. Nevertheless, many application areas that matter for daily life and society show only modest gains so far; a prominent case is diagnosing and treating dynamically evolving disease in intensive care.\n The common challenge is adapting complex systems to dynamic environments. Effective strategies must optimize outcomes in systems composed of strongly interacting functions while avoiding shared side effects; this requires reliable, self-adaptive modeling. These tasks align with building digital twins of highly complex systems whose mechanisms are not fully or quantitatively understood. It is therefore essential to develop methods for self-adapting AI models with minimal data and limited mechanistic knowledge. As this challenge extends beyond medicine, AI should demonstrate clear superiority in these settings before assuming broader decision-making roles.\n We identify the curse of dimensionality as a fundamental barrier to efficient self-adaptation and argue that monolithic foundation models face conceptual limits in overcoming it. As an alternative, we propose a decentralized architecture of interacting small agent networks (SANs). We focus on agents representing the specialized substructure of the system, where each agent covers only a subset of the full system functions. Drawing on mathematical results on the learning behavior of SANs and evidence from existing applications, we argue that swarm-learning in diverse swarms can enable self-adaptive SANs to deliver superior decision-making in dynamic environments compared with monolithic foundation models, though at the cost of reduced reproducibility in detail."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06349","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07119","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07119","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07119","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongki","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Dongki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehoon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jaehoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yonghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungmin","familyName":"Eum","name":"Eum, Sungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heesung","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Heesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinesh","familyName":"Manocha","name":"Manocha, Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoRe: Monocular Geometry Refinement via Graph Optimization for Cross-View Consistency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:11:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-27T06:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T01:25:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Monocular 3D foundation models offer an extensible solution for perception tasks, making them attractive for broader 3D vision applications. In this paper, we propose MoRe, a training-free Monocular Geometry Refinement method designed to improve cross-view consistency and achieve scale alignment. To induce inter-frame relationships, our method employs feature matching between frames to establish correspondences. Rather than applying simple least squares optimization on these matched points, we formulate a graph-based optimization framework that performs local planar approximation using the estimated 3D points and surface normals estimated by monocular foundation models. This formulation addresses the scale ambiguity inherent in monocular geometric priors while preserving the underlying 3D structure. We further demonstrate that MoRe not only enhances 3D reconstruction but also improves novel view synthesis, particularly in sparse view rendering scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07119","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-01T04:22:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfeng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaonan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Neural Architecture Design for Industrial Defect Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:37:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T03:13:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T01:20:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industrial surface defect detection (SDD) is critical for ensuring product quality and manufacturing reliability. Due to the diverse shapes and sizes of surface defects, SDD faces two main challenges: intraclass difference and interclass similarity. Existing methods primarily utilize manually designed models, which require extensive trial and error and often struggle to address both challenges effectively. To overcome this, we propose AutoNAD, an automated neural architecture design framework for SDD that jointly searches over convolutions, transformers, and multi-layer perceptrons. This hybrid design enables the model to capture both fine-grained local variations and long-range semantic context, addressing the two key challenges while reducing the cost of manual network design. To support efficient training of such a diverse search space, AutoNAD introduces a cross weight sharing strategy, which accelerates supernet convergence and improves subnet performance. Additionally, a searchable multi-level feature aggregation module (MFAM) is integrated to enhance multi-scale feature learning. Beyond detection accuracy, runtime efficiency is essential for industrial deployment. To this end, AutoNAD incorporates a latency-aware prior to guide the selection of efficient architectures. The effectiveness of AutoNAD is validated on three industrial defect datasets and further applied within a defect imaging and detection platform. Code is available at https://github.com/Yuxi104/AutoNAD."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:40Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-27T03:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07189","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07189","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07189","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fazle","familyName":"Rabbi","name":"Rabbi, Fazle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinqiu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Secure-Instruct: An Automated Pipeline for Synthesizing Instruction-Tuning Datasets Using LLMs for Secure Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:24:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T03:42:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:19:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although Large Language Models (LLMs) show promising solutions to automated code generation, they often produce insecure code that threatens software security. Current approaches (e.g., SafeCoder) to improve secure code generation are limited by small, imbalanced instruction-tuning datasets. In this work, we present Secure-Instruct, a novel pipeline that automatically synthesizes high-quality vulnerable and secure code examples and instruction-tunes LLMs to align task description and secure code generation abilities. We evaluate Secure-Instruct on four representative LLMs using two security-related benchmarks: our own CWEBench and the existing CWEval. CWEBench comprises 93 scenarios on 44 CWEs, all without overlap with Secure-Instruct's synthetic instruction-tuning dataset, while CWEval covers 31 CWEs with 119 manually verified security-critical tasks. We find that Secure-Instruct improves both security and functional correctness in code generation. On CWEBench, Secure-Instruct substantially improves secure code generation, giving a 28.5% increase on average in secure ratio over the pre-trained models and outperforms SafeCoder by 12.6%. On CWEval, Secure-Instruct achieves an increase of 157.3% for CodeLlama-7B and 46.4% for Mistral-7B in Func-Sec@1 over pretrained models, and significantly outperforms SafeCoder."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07189","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ieva","familyName":"Bagdonaviciute","name":"Bagdonaviciute, Ieva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Vineet","name":"Vineet, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Knowledge in Frontier Models: A Diagnostic Study of Failure Modes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T21:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive progress, it remains difficult to determine why they succeed or fail on complex reasoning tasks. Traditional benchmarks evaluate what models can answer correctly, not why they succeed or fail. In this work, we perform a failure-mode analysis of six frontier VLMs on three physics-based benchmarks - Physion, Physion++, and CLEVRER - by introducing custom subtests (for Physion and Physion++) and an integration of existing benchmark categories (for CLEVRER) to factor benchmark performance into distinct, testable capabilities. These subtests isolate perception (object, color, and occlusion recognition) and physics understanding (motion prediction and spatial reasoning), enabling us to test whether models attend to the correct entities and dynamics underlying their answers. Counterintuitively, subtest mastery correlates only weakly with benchmark accuracy: models often answer correctly without grounding in perception or physics. This suggests that current VLMs sometimes achieve benchmark scores for the wrong reasons, underscoring the need for diagnostics that expose hidden failure modes beyond aggregate metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures. Preprint. v2: Updated experiments and diagnostics; formatting fixes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":246,"totalPages":3,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29"}}